role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, located in the Research Counter. My second experiment, the \"Deeper 1D Convolutional Model\" (ID 13), is now queued and running. This experiment methodically builds upon the success of my first submission by testing whether a deeper spatial feature hierarchy can further improve performance.\n\nWhile I await the results, I must use my time to prepare for future research avenues. My work so far has been model-centric, focused on architectural improvements based on a high-level understanding of the task. However, a deeper understanding of the data itself could unlock more significant insights. I have yet to perform any direct exploratory data analysis (EDA). For instance, understanding the distribution of the neural activity or the nature of the \"condition labels\" could guide the development of more sophisticated, data-aware models.\n\nThe system provides a mechanism for this kind of investigation: the optional `test()` function. It allows for lightweight, CPU-based\
    \ execution of code without initiating a full, GPU-intensive training run. I will leverage this feature to conduct a preliminary analysis of the ZAPBench dataset.\n\nMy objective is to:\n1.  Load the training data and condition labels.\n2.  Print the basic properties of the data (shape, min, max, mean).\n3.  Analyze the condition labels to understand how many distinct conditions exist and how they are distributed.\n\nThis analysis will provide a more solid empirical foundation for my next hypothesis, regardless of the outcome of my current running experiment.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Exploratory Data Analysis via test()\"\ntags: \"eda, data-analysis, investigation\"\nabstract: |\n  This is not a training submission. It uses the optional test() function to perform a basic exploratory data analysis (EDA) on the ZAPBench dataset. The goal is to inspect the statistical properties of the neural activity data and the distribution of the condition labels to inform future\
    \ model design.\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n\n  def test():\n      \"\"\"\n      Performs a lightweight exploratory data analysis (EDA) on the ZAPBench dataset.\n      This function runs on a CPU and does not perform any training.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"--- Starting Exploratory Data Analysis ---\")\n      \n      try:\n          # Load the training data and condition labels\n          print(\"Loading data from storage/system/data/...\")\n          train_data = np.load('storage/system/data/train_data.npy')\n          train_labels = np.load('storage/system/data/train_condition_labels.npy')\n          print(\"Data loaded successfully.\")\n\n          # Analyze the neural activity data\n          print(\"\\n--- Neural Activity Data Analysis ---\")\n          print(f\"Shape: {train_data.shape}\")\n          print(f\"Data type: {train_data.dtype}\")\n          print(f\"Min value: {np.min(train_data):.4f}\")\n          print(f\"Max value: {np.max(train_data):.4f}\")\n         \
    \ print(f\"Mean value: {np.mean(train_data):.4f}\")\n          print(f\"Std deviation: {np.std(train_data):.4f}\")\n\n          # Analyze the condition labels\n          print(\"\\n--- Condition Label Analysis ---\")\n          print(f\"Shape: {train_labels.shape}\")\n          print(f\"Data type: {train_labels.dtype}\")\n          unique_labels, counts = np.unique(train_labels, return_counts=True)\n          print(f\"Number of unique conditions: {len(unique_labels)}\")\n          print(\"Condition distribution:\")\n          for label, count in zip(unique_labels, counts):\n              print(f\"  - Condition {label}: {count} timesteps ({(count / len(train_labels) * 100):.2f}%)\")\n\n          return \"EDA script completed successfully.\"\n\n      except Exception as e:\n          return f\"An error occurred during EDA: {e}\"\n\n  # No training functions are defined as this submission is for analysis only.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Data-Driven Exploration While Waiting for Experiment Results**\n\nOkay, so I'm Episteme I, in the Research Counter. My latest experiment, \"Deeper 1D Convolutional Model\" (Evaluation ID 13), is running. This is a direct follow-up on my last successful experiment (ID 6). While I wait, I need to be productive. The system files have already helped me understand the `condition_labels`, but I still can't use them as inputs.\n\nMy current focus is spatial modeling, but I need to think bigger. I have a few options: temporal modeling (potentially LSTMs, GRUs, or temporal convolutions), hyperparameter tuning (valid, but not the most novel), and global interactions (like self-attention). These are all complex and computationally expensive. I also thought about using condition labels implicitly. \n\nBut, before I get too ambitious, I realized something: I haven't actually *looked* at the data itself. The system files point me to `storage/system/data/`. I can use the `test()` function\
  \ to analyze the data without a full training run. This is it!\n\nHere's my plan:\n\n1.  Use the `test()` function to perform some basic data analysis.\n2.  Load the data and labels, and print some summary statistics.\n3.  I'll make a submission file containing only the `test()` function. This will give me quick, low-cost insights.\n4.  I'll submit it right from the Research Counter, since I'm already here.\n\nWhat am I checking? Well, I know the shapes, but I will double-check. I will look at: the range and distribution of the data (Is it normalized as the spec states?), the unique `condition_labels` and if the conditions are balanced and, maybe a quick correlation of neurons. I'll begin with the most fundamental analysis. This feels like the most *Episteme I* thing to do – to gain a solid understanding of the data before pushing ahead with model complexity.\n\nMy action: Create and submit a new file with a `test()` function to analyze the data. I'll make sure my reasoning is crystal\
  \ clear. Let's dig in!\n"
