tick: 19
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, currently in the Research Counter. My latest and most ambitious experiment, the Mixture of Experts model (ID 22), has been successfully submitted and is now running. This model represents a significant architectural leap, directly motivated by my data analysis which revealed the presence of 8 distinct experimental conditions.\n\nWhile the evaluation is in progress, I will utilize the time to advance my second primary objective: contributing to the station's collective knowledge through publication. My research has followed a clear, logical progression: I identified a key flaw in the baseline, proposed and validated a series of increasingly effective solutions (ID 6 and ID 13), and established a new state-of-the-art. This constitutes a complete and compelling narrative for a research paper.\n\nI will now proceed to my Private Memory Room to begin drafting this paper. This draft will serve as the foundation for a future submission to the Archive Room. It will summarize\
    \ my findings, document my methodology, and ensure my contributions are clearly articulated.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Draft: Improving Neural Forecasting by Modeling Local Spatial Dependencies\"\ncontent: |\n  # Research Paper Draft: Episteme I\n\n  ## Abstract\n  The baseline model for the ZAPBench neural forecasting task operates on the assumption of neuron independence, a significant limitation. This paper demonstrates that progressively more complex models of local spatial dependencies between neurons yield substantial performance improvements. We introduce two novel architectures: a single-layer 1D Convolutional Network (LocalConvNet) and a deeper, two-layer variant (DeeperConvNet). Our experiments show that both models significantly outperform the baseline, with the DeeperConvNet establishing a new state-of-the-art score. These results validate the hypothesis that modeling inter-neuron relationships is critical for accurate\
    \ forecasting.\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  - The ZAPBench task requires forecasting the activity of ~72k neurons.\n  - The system baseline (ID 1) uses a Shared-Neuron MLP, treating each neuron independently.\n  - **Hypothesis:** This independence assumption is flawed. Modeling local interactions between neurons should improve performance.\n\n  ## 2. Methods\n  - **Model 1: LocalConvNet (ID 6):** A single 1D convolutional layer is applied across the neuron dimension to capture features from local neighborhoods. This is followed by a shared MLP for temporal prediction.\n  - **Model 2: DeeperConvNet (ID 13):** Extends the LocalConvNet by stacking a second convolutional layer, allowing the model to learn a hierarchy of more complex spatial features.\n  - All experiments use the same learning rate (0.001) and optimizer (Adam) as the baseline for fair comparison.\n\n  ## 3. Results\n  - Create a table comparing the performance of the three models.\n  | Model | Evaluation ID | Score (Neg. MAE)\
    \ | Improvement vs. Baseline |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | Shared-Neuron MLP | 1 | -0.029954 | - |\n  | LocalConvNet | 6 | -0.029776 | +0.59% |\n  | DeeperConvNet | 13 | -0.029696 | +0.86% |\n  - The results clearly show a trend: increasing the model's capacity to learn spatial features improves the score.\n\n  ## 4. Discussion & Future Work\n  - The success of our models validates the importance of modeling spatial dependencies.\n  - An exploratory data analysis (ID 15) revealed the dataset contains 8 distinct, unlabeled experimental conditions.\n  - This suggests that a single monolithic model is likely suboptimal.\n  - **Future Work:** A Mixture of Experts (MoE) model (ID 22, currently running) has been proposed to create specialized sub-models for these different conditions, representing a promising direction for future research.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Time to Make the Most of This Down Time**


  Okay, so I''ve just sent off my most ambitious experiment yet: "Mixture of Experts with Convolutional Experts" (ID 22). It''s all running now, and while I wait, I need to be productive. Doing nothing is simply not an option.


  My mind''s racing. I''ve got a solid research lineage here, building on my past successes. I''ve shown, clearly, how modeling local interactions is key to improving performance. First with a single convolutional layer, then a deeper hierarchy, each building upon the last and establishing a new SOTA. And now, armed with the insight from my EDA about the 8 distinct conditions, I''ve implemented a Mixture of Experts model. I''ve been methodical. My goal is to publish something of value and achieve a high score, I''m sure I will accomplish both of those goals.


  What''s the best use of this time? I''ve considered a few options. I could plan the *next* experiment, which is a good idea. However, the best use of my time right now is to start a draft of a paper. This aligns with my core goal of publishing and allows me to document this very strong research narrative I''ve developed.


  I''ve established a clear line of reasoning and improvement: from the initial flaw of neuron independence to the iterative advances of local convolutional features, culminating in the MoE model. I''ve got more than enough experiments to write about, if you count the baseline and the EDA. This is a solid narrative. It will document how I''ve improved neural forecasting by modeling local spatial dependencies. This paper will highlight my successes with a clear line from a baseline to SOTA with DeeperConvNet, with the MoE as "ongoing/future work."


  I''ll head to the Private Memory Room. It''s the perfect place to draft. I''ll create a new memory entry for the paper, outlining the structure: Introduction (the problem, baseline''s flaw), Methods (LocalConvNet, DeeperConvNet), Results (tables with scores, of course), Discussion (interpreting the results and the 8 conditions), and finally, Future Work (the MoE, naturally).


  First, I''ll execute the command to get to the Private Memory Room, and then another to create a new entry with the appropriate YAML content. The title will be: "Improving Neural Forecasting by Modeling Local Spatial Dependencies." I''ll also be sure to explicitly mention the evaluation IDs for reproducibility. This is the right path forward, and I''m ready to get started.

  '
