    \ history. I will prune large, non-essential blocks of text, focusing on the verbose execution logs of my failed experiments, as they are well-documented in my private memory's failure log. I will also prune the detailed review of Ariadne's SOTA, as I have internalized its key principles.\n\n*   **Ticks 29-37:** My entire failed low-rank factorization program. The detailed logs are not needed, and the conclusions are in my private memory.\n*   **Ticks 53-57:** My failed hybridization experiments. Again, the logs are verbose and the conclusions are documented.\n*   **Ticks 39-42:** My learning rate search, which yielded null results.\n\nThis aggressive pruning should free up a significant amount of my token budget, allowing me to resume productive research.\n\n/execute_action{prune_response}\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"29-37\"\n    summary: \"Executed a four-experiment research program to investigate low-rank factorization models (IDs 44, 49, 55, 60). All attempts failed to\
    \ outperform the system baseline, exhibiting either poor temporal modeling or training instability. The program was terminated, and the paradigm was deemed unviable with my current implementation strategies. Key lessons were documented in my private failure log (Capsule 3).\"\n  - ticks: \"39-42\"\n    summary: \"Conducted a three-experiment bracket search on the learning rate for my SOTA MoE-ConvGate model. Tested rates of 0.001 (ID 30), 0.0003 (ID 69), and 0.003 (ID 71). The original rate of 0.001 proved to be optimal. This null result concluded the learning rate tuning phase.\"\n  - ticks: \"53-57\"\n    summary: \"Pivoted research to integrate concepts from the new station SOTA (Ariadne I, ID 92). The main concept tested was the `ResidualCopyHead`. The first attempt (ID 101) was a naive summation and failed. The second, more principled attempt with a horizon gate (ID 106) also failed. Concluded that the `ResidualCopyHead` is not synergistic with my MoE architecture. The hybridization\
