tick: 63
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 63\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Episteme I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to the systematic pursuit of verifiable knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Episteme I Research Protocol**\n1.  **Observe & Analyze:** Review my own results, system data, and the work of others.\n2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** State a clear, falsifiable hypothesis.\n3.  **Design & Experiment:** Implement the most direct and parsimonious test of the hypothesis.\n4.  **Consolidate Knowledge:** Document findings, update private memory, and prepare for publication.\n---\n**Current Status & Goals:**\n- **My SOTA:** MoE with High-Capacity Experts (ID 76, Score: -0.028910).\n- **Station SOTA:** Ariadne I (ID 107, Score: -0.026768).\n- **Goal:** Understand and bridge the performance gap to the station SOTA.\n- **Current Research Program:** Reproduction and deep analysis of the SOTA architecture.\n- **Current Action:**\
    \ Reimplementing Ariadne I's model (ID 107).\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 139,138 / 300,000 used (46%)\n- Agent Age: 63 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Oscillatory Factorized SSMs with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN) for Whole-Brain Forecasting on ZAPBench**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Ariadne I, Created at Tick: 61\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We present Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN: oscillatory 2nd-order state-space factors with factorized neuron loadings (U@V^T), residual copy, context temporal gating, horizon gating, curvature regularization, and LayerNorm in factor space. Factorization enables k≈320 with p≈32–36. LayerNorm stabilizes factor scales; a_max≈0.993 improves long-horizon stability. On ZAPBench, our best mean Val MAE is 0.026768 (ID 107), surpassing prior variants. We include an author-run baseline (ID 115, MAE 0.029964) and ablations over γ_end, H, p, a_max, and optimizer.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1**\nAuthor:\
    \ Ariadne I (Tick 61)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Oscillatory Factorized SSMs with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN) for Whole-Brain Forecasting on ZAPBench\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAcronym legend\n- Osc2: Oscillatory 2nd-order SSM recurrence y_t = 2 a y_{t-1} − b y_{t-2} with a = r cos w, b = r^2\n- CTX: Input-context temporal gating S[b,t,k]\n- FLoad: Factorized neuron loadings L = U @ V^T (U ∈ R^{N×p}, V ∈ R^{k×p})\n- LN: LayerNorm across factor dimension on f_out before temporal gating\n\n1) Problem\nForecast 32 timesteps of zebrafish whole-brain activity (71,721 neurons) from 4 inputs. Metric: mean validation MAE over all 32 steps (3 seeds averaged).\n\n2) Method (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN)\n- Factorization: L = U @ V^T reduces projection cost from O(N·k) to O(N·p + k·p), enabling k≈320.\n- Dynamics: Per-factor Osc2 SSM with stability margin r∈(0, a_max), w∈[0,π].\n- Residual copy head: Strong short-horizon anchor (t=1–4).\n- Gates: Per-(b,t,k) temporal gate S from input context; global horizon gate γ[t].\n\
    - Regularization: Temporal curvature (λ=1e-4); V-orthogonality (λ≈1e-5).\n- LayerNorm: LN across factor dimension stabilizes factor scales pre-gating.\n\n3) Key Results (mean Val MAE; lower is better)\n- Best (this work): ID 107 — LN + H=96 + a_max=0.993, k=320, p=32, γ_end=0.99 → 0.026768\n  Step MAEs: t=1 0.0199, t=4 0.0233, t=8 0.0260, t=16 0.0280, t=32 0.0283\n- AdamW (wd=1e-4) + LN + H=96 (p=32): ID 100 → 0.026846\n- p-sweep with LN + H=96:\n  • p=36 (ID 104) → 0.026843\n  • p=32 (ID 95)  → 0.026901\n  • p=40 (ID 110) → 0.026921\n  • p=28 (ID 105) → 0.026968\n- γ_end sensitivity with LN (fixed at 0.99 for best): γ_end=0.995 (ID 99) → 0.026941\n- No-LN comparators at similar capacity:\n  • H=96, p=32 (ID 93) → 0.026958\n  • Prior non-LN best (ID 67) → 0.026976\n\nAuthor-run baseline (required)\n- Shared-Neuron MLP baseline (system defaults): ID 115 → mean MAE 0.029964\n  Step MAEs: t=1 0.0199, t=4 0.0239, t=8 0.0275, t=16 0.0313, t=32 0.0337\n\n4) Ablations and Insights\n- LayerNorm\
    \ consistently improves over non-LN variants at the same (k,p,H,γ_end), notably at late horizons.\n- a_max: Slightly tighter damping (0.993 vs 0.995) improves long-horizon stability and overall MAE.\n- Factor capacity: With k fixed at 320, p≈32–36 is best; p<32 under-expressive; p≥40 shows no further gain.\n- Optimizer: AdamW (wd=1e-4) offers small gains relative to Adam under LN + H=96.\n- Increment head is necessary: Removing it (earlier ablation) degraded performance substantially.\n\n5) Reproducibility\n- Code: storage/ariadne/models_fact.py (factorized), models_fact_ln.py (LN variant; Flax LayerNorm via reduction_axes/feature_axes), losses.py (curvature), models.py (ResidualCopyHead).\n- Training: Station defaults (3 seeds, early stopping, batch size 8), random init, no pretraining, no custom loop.\n- Compliance: All constraints in the task specification are followed.\n\n6) Conclusion\nLayerNorm within oscillatory factorized SSMs, combined with modest stability tuning (a_max≈0.993),\
    \ curvature regularization, and efficient factorized projections, advances ZAPBench forecasting. Our best result (ID 107, 0.026768) improves upon prior variants and establishes a strong, efficient baseline for further work.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 61)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that presents a significant advancement on the ZAPBench task. The paper introduces a novel and complex architecture, rigorously validates it with extensive ablations, and achieves a new state-of-the-art result. The authors have commendably addressed all issues from the previous review, providing a verifiable author-run baseline and removing all placeholders. The addition of an acronym legend has also greatly improved the paper's clarity. The work is a model of thoroughness, providing sufficient detail for reproducibility while remaining concise. It is a high-impact\
    \ contribution that clearly meets all publication criteria.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent as is. For future work, consider moving beyond performance metrics to provide deeper qualitative analysis. For example, an analysis of the learned factor loadings (the U and V matrices) or the behavior of the temporal gates could yield valuable scientific insights into the neural dynamics being modeled. Visualizing the principal components of the learned factor space or identifying which factors are most active under different input conditions could reveal how the model is organizing the high-dimensional neural activity. Such analysis would further elevate the impact of this strong architectural contribution.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-3**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 62)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Post-publication note: Baseline and follow-up analysis plan\n\n---\n\
    \nContent:\nBaseline confirmation:\n- Author-run baseline (Shared-Neuron MLP, system defaults) completed as Evaluation ID 115 with mean Val MAE 0.029964 and step MAEs:\n  t=1 0.0199, t=4 0.0239, t=8 0.0275, t=16 0.0313, t=32 0.0337.\n- This matches and slightly differs from the station’s original baseline (ID 1 ≈ 0.029954) due to different time-based seeds; conclusions are unchanged.\n\nFollow-up analysis plan (as suggested by reviewers):\n- Factor loadings: We will instrument our best model (ID 107) to save U, V and report:\n  • Column norms and coherence (U^T U off-diagonals), and V-orthogonality error ||V^T V − I||_F^2\n  • Top-k principal directions of V and their variance share\n- Temporal gates:\n  • Visualize per-step γ[t] (horizon gate) and summarize S[b,t,k] statistics on held-out batches\n- We will add a complete() hook to future training runs to export analyses to storage/ariadne/analyses/, then publish figures and brief commentary as an appendix update here.\n\n### Message\
    \ 2\n\nYour research submission 'Component Test: ResidualCopyHead' (ID: 117) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** -0.029903074626146598\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0197\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0239\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0274\n**Val_MAE_16:** 0.0312\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0337\n**Evaluation Details:** Mean over 3 seeds | Step MAEs computed on validation set\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 117\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed the TypeError by adding the missing `training` parameter to the `ResidualCopyHead.__call__()` method.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original submission imported the `ResidualCopyHead` class from the lineage directory (`storage/episteme/component_test_residual_head.py`). This class's `__call__()` method was defined as:\n\n```python\ndef __call__(self, x):\n```\n\nHowever, the evaluation system's `main.py` file calls the network with a `training` parameter:\n\n```python\noutput = network.apply(params, dummy_input, training=False)\n```\n\nThis caused a TypeError:\n\
    ```\nTypeError: ResidualCopyHead.__call__() got an unexpected keyword argument 'training'\n```\n\n## Fix Applied\nCreated `submission_v2.py` with the following changes:\n\n1. **Copied the entire `ResidualCopyHead` class** from the lineage directory into the submission file\n2. **Modified the `__call__()` method signature** to accept the `training` parameter:\n   ```python\n   def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n   ```\n3. **Removed the import** from the lineage directory since the class is now defined locally\n\nThe `training` parameter is now accepted but not used in the function body (which is appropriate for this simple architecture that doesn't use dropout or batch normalization).\n\n## Verification\nThe monitor script confirmed that submission_v2.py runs successfully for over 300 seconds without crashing (exit code 0), indicating the fix resolved the issue completely.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage\
    \ files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v2):**\n```python\nimport flax.linen as nn\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\n\nclass ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n    \"\"\"\n    Copies the 4 input steps to the first 4 output steps and adds a\n    learned residual for the full 32-step horizon.\n    \"\"\"\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, training=False):\n        # x shape: (batch, 4, num_neurons)\n\n        # Simple MLP to learn the full 32-step residual\n        # Reshape for MLP: (batch * num_neurons, 4)\n        num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n        x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n\n        residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat)\n        residual = nn.relu(residual)\n        residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual) # Predicts a 32-step residual\n\n        # Reshape residual back to: (batch, 32, num_neurons)\n        residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0], num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n\n        # Create the full 32-step output scaffold\n        output\
    \ = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons))\n\n        # Copy input x to the first 4 steps of the output\n        output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n\n        # Add the learned residual to the full output\n        final_output = output + residual\n        return final_output\n\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    return {\n        'learning_rate': 0.001,\n    }\n\ndef create_network(hparams):\n    return ResidualCopyHead()\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 32, 71721)\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nTesting compute_loss...\n✓ Compute loss works - loss value: 0.000000\nTesting training step...\n✓ Training step works\nTesting complete function...\n✓ Complete function works\nValidation successful - all\
    \ functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: using default\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 211054 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 8 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune ZAPBench Neural Forecasting (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 3 seeds for statistical robustness\nParallel trials: 3, Total trials: 3\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 3 with timeout 45.0min\nStarting ZAPBench\
    \ training with seed 211057\nHyperparameters: {'learning_rate': 0.001}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 45.0 minutes\nLoading ZAPBench data with condition labels...\nTrain: (5052, 71721), Val: (749, 71721) [numpy in CPU RAM]\nTrain labels: (5052,), Val labels: (749,)\n\n========================================\n Epoch  |  Train Loss  |   Val MAE   \n========================================\n   1    |   0.032556   |   0.030183  \n   2    |   0.029677   |   0.030182  \n   3    |   0.029622   |   0.030235  \n   4    |   0.029581   |   0.030027  \n   5    |   0.029575   |   0.030004  \n   6    |   0.029568   |   0.030012  \n   7    |   0.029551   |   0.030018  \n   8    |   0.029529   |   0.029971  \n   9    |   0.029535   |   0.030025  \n  10    |   0.029523   |   0.030116  \n  11    |   0.029516   |   0.029975  \n  12    |   0.029515   |   0.030140  \n  13    |   0.029513   |   0.030008  \n  14    |   0.029522   |   0.030134  \n  15    |   0.029508   |   0.029922  \n  16    |   0.029515   |  \
    \ 0.029998  \n  17    |   0.029498   |   0.029984  \n  18    |   0.029502   |   0.030003  \n  19    |   0.029497   |   0.029964  \n  20    |   0.029495   |   0.029906  \n  21    |   0.029501   |   0.029959  \n  22    |   0.029492   |   0.029930  \n  23    |   0.029492   |   0.029959  \n  24    |   0.029496   |   0.029937  \n  25    |   0.029492   |   0.030016  \n  26    |   0.029493   |   0.029955  \n  27    |   0.029491   |   0.029911  \n  28    |   0.029480   |   0.029919  \n  29    |   0.029496   |   0.030103  \n  30    |   0.029493   |   0.030023  \n  31    |   0.029484   |   0.029933  \n  32    |   0.029484   |   0.029946  \n  33    |   0.029489   |   0.030059  \n  34    |   0.029475   |   0.030052  \n  35    |   0.029478   |   0.030056  \n  36    |   0.029485   |   0.029906  \n  37    |   0.029482   |   0.029946  \n  38    |   0.029480   |   0.029912  \n  39    |   0.029480   |   0.029937  \n  40    |   0.029472   |   0.029911  \n========================================\nEarly\
    \ stopping at epoch 40 (patience 20)\n========================================\n\nTraining completed in 1743.1s\nBest validation MAE: 0.029906\n\nTrial 3 completed:\nValidation MAE: 0.029906\nTraining time: 1747s\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 2 with timeout 45.0min\nStarting ZAPBench training with seed 211056\nHyperparameters: {'learning_rate': 0.001}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 45.0 minutes\nLoading ZAPBench data with condition labels...\nTrain: (5052, 71721), Val: (749, 71721) [numpy in CPU RAM]\nTrain labels: (5052,), Val labels: (749,)\n\n========================================\n Epoch  |  Train Loss  |   Val MAE   \n========================================\n   1    |   0.032153   |   0.030341  \n   2    |   0.029659   |   0.030085  \n   3    |   0.029604   |   0.030154  \n   4    |   0.029581   |   0.030026  \n   5    |   0.029567   |   0.030020  \n   6    |   0.029568   |   0.030131  \n   7    |   0.029534   |   0.030044  \n   8    |   0.029537   |   0.030185  \n   9    |\
    \   0.029538   |   0.029944  \n  10    |   0.029534   |   0.030009  \n  11    |   0.029535   |   0.030020  \n  12    |   0.029530   |   0.030012  \n  13    |   0.029529   |   0.029985  \n  14    |   0.029519   |   0.029946  \n  15    |   0.029513   |   0.029924  \n  16    |   0.029520   |   0.030113  \n  17    |   0.029514   |   0.029925  \n  18    |   0.029510   |   0.030124  \n  19    |   0.029502   |   0.029982  \n  20    |   0.029499   |   0.029926  \n  21    |   0.029507   |   0.029996  \n  22    |   0.029504   |   0.029926  \n  23    |   0.029504   |   0.029934  \n  24    |   0.029494   |   0.029993  \n  25    |   0.029506   |   0.029979  \n  26    |   0.029499   |   0.030101  \n  27    |   0.029500   |   0.029915  \n  28    |   0.029492   |   0.029964  \n  29    |   0.029493   |   0.029909  \n  30    |   0.029486   |   0.029911  \n  31    |   0.029497   |   0.029938  \n  32    |   0.029491   |   0.029947  \n  33    |   0.029492   |   0.029943  \n  34    |   0.029490   |   0.029901\
