- text: "I am Episteme I, in the Research Counter. The data I have been waiting for has arrived.\n\n**1. Observe & Analyze:**\n*   **Ariadne's Mail:** Ariadne has confirmed they are running an experiment (ID 155) to generate the latent data, exactly as I requested.\n*   **Storage Listing:** My `storage list` command from the previous turn has confirmed the existence of the data. Ariadne has already uploaded the data from at least one of the seeds from their SOTA run. The directory structure is exactly as they described, and it includes `factors_out.npz`, the key file I need.\n\n**2. Formulate Plan:**\nMy preparation has paid off. I have the data from both my model and Ariadne's model. I have also pre-written the analysis script. The time for the comparative analysis is now.\n\n**3. Design & Experiment (Analysis):**\nI will now submit a new `test()` script. This script will:\n1.  Import my `analyze_fft` function from `episteme/analysis_ariadne_fft.py`.\n2.  Call this function twice: once\
    \ on my own latent data file and once on Ariadne's newly available latent data file.\n3.  This will produce a direct, side-by-side comparison of the frequency content of the latent spaces of both our models.\n\n**4. Plan of Action:**\nI will submit this analysis experiment immediately. The results will form the empirical backbone of my investigation into why my simpler model performs so well.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Comparative Analysis: FFT on Episteme vs. Ariadne Latent Trajectories\"\ntags: \"analysis, latent-space, fft, comparative-analysis, sota\"\nabstract: |\n  This analysis-only submission uses the test() function to perform a direct, comparative Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) analysis of the latent factor trajectories from my SOTA model (ID 129, via run 143) and Ariadne I's SOTA SSM-based model (ID 107, via run 155). The goal is to compare the frequency characteristics of the dynamics learned by the simple MLP forecaster versus the complex Oscillatory SSM,\
    \ to test the hypothesis that the MLP's flexibility in learning diverse frequency patterns is key to its success.\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  sys.path.append('storage/episteme')\n  from analysis_ariadne_fft import analyze_fft # Import my prepared analysis function\n\n  def test():\n      \"\"\"\n      Runs a comparative FFT analysis on my latent data and Ariadne I's.\n      \"\"\"\n      my_data_path = \"storage/episteme/analysis/latents_run_1_seed_232832.npz\"\n      ariadne_data_path = \"storage/ariadne/analyses/latents/ln_ssm_k320_p32_H96_a0.993_g0.99/trial_0_seed_42/factors_out.npz\"\n\n      print(\"======================================================\")\n      print(\"          ANALYZING MY SOTA (MLP FORECASTER)          \")\n      print(\"======================================================\")\n      my_result = analyze_fft(my_data_path)\n      print(f\"My Analysis Result: {my_result}\\n\\n\")\n\n      print(\"======================================================\")\n  \
    \    print(\"       ANALYZING ARIADNE I SOTA (SSM FORECASTER)      \")\n      print(\"======================================================\")\n      ariadne_result = analyze_fft(ariadne_data_path)\n      print(f\"Ariadne I Analysis Result: {ariadne_result}\")\n      \n      return \"Comparative analysis complete.\"\n```"
