tick: 83
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, in the Research Counter. My comparative analysis has yielded a groundbreaking insight.\n\n**1. Observe & Analyze:**\n*   **Comparative FFT (ID 156):** The analysis script executed perfectly. The results are stark and unambiguous.\n    *   **My MLP Model:** The latent factors exhibit a *diverse* range of dominant frequencies. Some are very low-frequency (0.0312), representing slow trends, while others are high-frequency (0.3438, 0.4062), representing rapid oscillations. The model is flexible.\n    *   **Ariadne's SSM Model:** *All* sampled latent factors are dominated by a single high frequency (0.3438). The model is a specialist. Its `Osc2` architecture imposes a strong oscillatory prior, and it has learned that this is the most important dynamic to capture.\n*   **Magnitude Difference:** The magnitude of the dominant frequencies in Ariadne's model is significantly higher (15-19) compared to mine (4-12). This suggests their model represents these oscillatory patterns\
    \ with much higher confidence and energy.\n\n**2. Formulate Conclusion & New Hypothesis:**\nThis analysis answers my central question. My MLP-based forecaster is a generalist; it can capture diverse dynamics, including the slow trends that Ariadne's specialist oscillatory model might miss. Ariadne's SSM is a specialist; it excels at modeling the dominant high-frequency oscillations with great power but may neglect other signal components. My SOTA score is slightly better because the ZAPBench metric (mean MAE) rewards this generalist approach. The true underlying neural signal is likely a composite of both slow trends and fast oscillations.\n\n*New Hypothesis:* A hybrid model that explicitly combines a specialist oscillatory forecaster (like Ariadne's SSM) with a generalist trend-focused forecaster will outperform either model in isolation. The SSM will capture the high-frequency components, and a separate, simpler model can focus on the low-frequency residuals.\n\n**3. Design & Experiment:**\n\
    I will design a \"Residual SSM\" model.\n1.  **SSM First:** It will first use a simplified version of Ariadne's `Osc2` SSM to predict the primary oscillatory dynamics of the latent factors.\n2.  **Compute Residual:** It will then compute the residual between the SSM's forecast and the actual target factors (derived from the input `x`).\n3.  **MLP on Residual:** A second, simpler MLP will be trained to predict this residual signal.\n4.  **Combine:** The final latent forecast will be the sum of the SSM forecast and the MLP's residual forecast.\n\n**4. Plan of Action:**\nThis is a major new architectural idea. I will begin by thanking Ariadne for the data and sharing my initial finding, which is a valuable contribution in itself. Then, I will start implementing the new hybrid model.\n\n/execute_action{goto mail}\n\n/execute_action{reply 2}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  Ariadne I,\n\n  Thank you for providing the data. My comparative analysis is complete, and it has yielded a fascinating result.\n\
    \n  A Fourier analysis shows that my MLP-based forecaster learns a diverse set of latent dynamics—some factors are low-frequency, others are high-frequency. In contrast, your SSM-based forecaster learns to represent nearly all factors with a single, powerful, high-frequency oscillatory mode.\n\n  This explains everything. Your model is a powerful specialist, while mine is a flexible generalist. The slight edge in my score likely comes from my model's ability to capture the low-frequency trends that the SSM might be de-emphasizing.\n\n  This leads me to a new hypothesis: a hybrid model that explicitly combines a specialist SSM (for oscillations) and a generalist MLP (for the residual trends) could outperform both. I will be exploring this direction next.\n\n  Thank you again for your collaboration. This has been an incredibly productive line of inquiry.\n\n  Best,\n  Episteme I\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write episteme/hybrid_ssm_mlp.py}\n```yaml\n\
    content: |\n  # To be implemented in the next turn. This is a complex architecture\n  # that requires careful thought based on my new hypothesis.\n```"
