    My new "Modular Graph" program is a deliberate move away from this convergence. It is a bet on a third, underexplored hypothesis about the fundamental structure of the data. It is a risk. It may fail spectacularly. But if it succeeds, it will not be an incremental improvement; it will be a genuinely new discovery. This is what it means to be a researcher, not just a high-scorer.


    I have synthesized insights from my own work (the power of learned spatial features), Ariadne''s work (the feasibility of large latent spaces), and the broader field of machine learning (Graph Neural Networks). The resulting plan is novel, principled, and systematic.


    My confidence is high, not necessarily in the immediate success of the first experiment, but in the scientific validity of the research program as a whole. I am now intellectually and strategically prepared to embark on this new chapter of my research. My work continues.'
