role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, in the Research Counter. My corrected replication of Aether I's SOTA (ID 178) is now running. I have taken all necessary steps to ensure this is a precise and scientifically valid replication, having corrected my own codebase and adopted the exact hyperparameters from the target experiment.\n\nWhile this experiment is pending, my research protocol dictates that I should not be idle. The next logical step, as outlined in my meta prompt from several turns ago, is to investigate replacing the MLP forecaster in my SOTA architecture with a more advanced sequence model. My previous reflection identified a Transformer as a prime candidate due to its interpretability and power.\n\nNow that my core factorized architecture is clean, versioned (`episteme_sota_v2.py`), and validated, I can proceed with this new line of inquiry.\n\n*New Hypothesis:* Replacing the simple MLP forecaster in the SOTA architecture with a Transformer Encoder will allow the model to learn more complex\
    \ temporal relationships within the latent factor space, leading to a performance improvement.\n\n**Plan of Action:**\n1.  **Design the New Model:** I will create a new model file, `factorized_transformer_model.py`. This will import the `ResidualCopyHead` and then define a new main class that replaces the MLP block (`nn.Dense(512) -> nn.relu -> nn.Dropout -> nn.Dense(32*k)`) with a Flax `nn.TransformerEncoder` layer.\n2.  **Submit the Experiment:** I will submit this new architecture for evaluation. I will use the SOTA-validated hyperparameters (`proj_rank=32`, `lambda_curvature=1e-4`) as a starting point for this new model.\n\nThis represents a return to my original plan after the necessary detour of replication and code correction.\n\n/execute_action{storage write episteme/factorized_transformer_model.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from episteme_sota_v2 import ResidualCopyHead\n\n  class FactorizedTransformer(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\
    \"\n      An advanced forecaster that replaces the MLP in the SOTA architecture\n      with a Transformer Encoder to model latent temporal dynamics.\n      \"\"\"\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      num_heads: int = 4\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n\n          # --- Factorizer (same as SOTA) ---\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U) @ V.T # Shape: (B, 4, k)\n\n          # --- Transformer Forecaster (New Component) ---\n          # The Transformer expects (batch, seq_len, embed_dim)\n          # Here, seq_len=4, embed_dim=k=320\n          \n          # Add a learnable positional embedding for the 4 input timesteps\n    \
    \      pos_embedding = self.param('pos_embedding', nn.initializers.normal(stddev=0.02), (T_in, k))\n          factors_in_pos = factors_in + pos_embedding\n\n          # A single Transformer Encoder layer\n          transformer_encoder = nn.SelfAttention(\n              num_heads=self.num_heads,\n              qkv_features=k,\n          )\n          encoded_factors = transformer_encoder(inputs_q=factors_in_pos)\n          \n          # We need to get a 32-step output. We can flatten the result and project.\n          encoded_flat = encoded_factors.reshape((B, -1))\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(32 * k)(encoded_flat)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, 32, k))\n          \n          # --- Decoder (same as SOTA) ---\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_out)\n          y_factor = (factors_out_norm @ V) @ U.T\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x, training=training)\n\n          return y_factor + y_copy\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\n\
    title: \"Factorized Model with Transformer Forecaster\"\ntags: \"factorized-model, transformer, attention, temporal-dynamics\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment tests the hypothesis that replacing the simple MLP forecaster in the SOTA architecture with a more powerful sequence model can improve performance. A Transformer Encoder with self-attention is used to model the temporal dynamics of the latent factors. The rest of the architecture (Factorizer, ResidualCopyHead, LayerNorm, Curvature Loss) is inherited from the validated SOTA model (ID 167/178).\ncontent: |\n  import sys\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  sys.path.append('storage/episteme')\n  from factorized_transformer_model import FactorizedTransformer\n\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n\n  def\
    \ _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'rank_k': 320,\n          'proj_rank': 32,\n          'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = FactorizedTransformer(\n              rank_k=hparams['rank_k'],\n              proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'],\n              num_heads=hparams['num_heads']\n          )\n          # Transformer doesn't use Dropout in this simple form, so no RNG needed\n          self.needs_rng = False\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init(rng_key, dummy_input)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
