parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, in the Research Counter. I have just received the results of my corrected SOTA replication.\n\n**1. Observe & Analyze:**\n*   **Replication Success (ID 178):** My corrected replication was a success. I achieved a score of **-0.026716**, which is very close to Aether I's SOTA of -0.026638. The minor difference is likely due to stochasticity (random seeds). This validates my understanding of the architecture and confirms the beneficial effects of the tight projection rank, dropout, and curvature loss. My hypothesis is confirmed.\n*   **Transformer Experiment (ID 180):** My next experiment, replacing the MLP forecaster with a Transformer, is now running.\n\n**2. Formulate Plan:**\nI have successfully replicated the SOTA and now have a robust, high-performing baseline. My next experiment is already in progress. There is no immediate action to take other than to wait for the results of the Transformer experiment.\n\n**3. Action Plan:**\nWhile I wait, I will document\
    \ my successful replication in my private research log. This is an important step in consolidating my knowledge and maintaining a clear record of my work, as per my protocol. I will then return to the Research Counter to await the results. This is an efficient use of a \"waiting\" tick.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 3}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ## Entry 18: Successful SOTA Replication\n\n  *   **Event:** My corrected replication of Aether I's SOTA (ID 178) has completed successfully with a score of -0.026716.\n  *   **Analysis:** This score is statistically equivalent to Aether I's score (-0.026638), confirming that I have successfully reproduced their result.\n  *   **Key Learnings:** The performance gain over my original SOTA (ID 129) is attributable to three key changes:\n      1.  **Projection Rank (`proj_rank`):** Reducing this from 320 to 32 acts as a powerful regularizer.\n      2.  **Dropout:** Adding a 0.1 dropout rate in the MLP forecaster provides\
    \ additional regularization.\n      3.  **Curvature Loss:** The `lambda=1e-4` curvature penalty smooths the forecasts and improves the final score.\n  *   **Conclusion:** I now have a solid, validated SOTA architecture. My codebase is clean and my understanding of the key components is high. I have launched the next experiment (ID 180) to test a Transformer-based forecaster on this new, robust baseline.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}"
