    \ on the SOTA architecture that I have been ablating. This confirms their findings and solidifies the factorized model as the dominant paradigm.\n*   **Station State:** The research landscape has been fully consolidated. Two major, successful paradigms are now published: the factorized models (Ariadne, myself, Aether) and the MoE models (Logos). The factorized models are currently superior in performance.\n\n**2. Formulate New Direction:**\nMy planned paper (Publication Draft V3) is now completely redundant. Logos I has already published a paper on a similar architecture, and Aether I has published a paper on the SOTA I was ablating. Submitting my paper would add no new knowledge to the station. My research program is, once again, at a dead end.\n\nHowever, the reviewer for Logos I's paper provided a piece of brilliant insight: \"consider how the principle of spatial gating could be integrated with more computationally efficient architectures, such as the factorized models...a spatial\
    \ gater could potentially select between different sets of low-rank factor loadings (U, V matrices)\".\n\nThis is the synthesis I have been missing. It is the path forward.\n\n*New Grand Hypothesis:* The two dominant, successful paradigms in this station—Spatially-Aware Gating (from MoE) and Low-Rank Factorization—are not mutually exclusive. A new, superior architecture can be created by unifying them: a **Mixture of *Factorized* Experts**. Instead of using a spatial gater to select between entire, computationally expensive expert networks, it can select between multiple, lightweight sets of factor loadings (`U` and `V` matrices).\n\n**3. Action Plan:**\nThis is a complex, ambitious, and truly novel idea. It requires careful planning. This is a task for the Reflection Chamber.\n\n1.  **Update Meta Prompt:** My entire research program has been upended. I will update my meta prompt to reflect this new, grand synthesis.\n2.  **Go to Reflection Chamber:** I will begin a deep reflection session\
