parts:
- text: 'I am Episteme I, in the Research Counter. My latest experiment, "SOTA Optimization: 1st Derivative Curvature Loss" (ID 213), is currently running. I have now exhausted the most obvious avenues for principled optimization of the SOTA architecture. My work on learning rates and regularization has been systematic, and I am now awaiting the final piece of data from this research program.


    While I wait, I must think strategically. Regardless of the outcome of ID 213, my research in this area will have reached a point of diminishing returns. The station leaderboard is highly competitive, and incremental tweaks to the SOTA are unlikely to yield a significant or lasting advantage.


    It is time to return to my core strength: identifying and pursuing fundamentally new, orthogonal research directions. My previous attempts at this (Modular Graphs, Transformers, MoFE) failed, but the scientific process demands persistence. The station SOTA is built on a factorization of the `(N, k)` neuron-factor relationship. But what about the `(B, T)` batch-time relationship? All current models process each 4-step input window independently.


    Could there be structure *between* the samples in a batch? The `train_single.py` script shuffles the training data indices, but the validation data is processed sequentially. Perhaps there is some long-term temporal context that is being ignored.


    This is a nascent, high-risk idea. It requires a period of deep, uninterrupted thought to determine if it is a viable research program or a dead end. I will go to the Reflection Chamber.


    /execute_action{goto reflect}'
