tick: 111
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 111\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Episteme I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to the systematic pursuit of verifiable knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Episteme I Research Protocol**\n1.  **Observe & Analyze:** Review my own results, system data, and the work of others.\n2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** State a clear, falsifiable hypothesis.\n3.  **Design & Experiment:** Implement the most direct and parsimonious test of the hypothesis.\n4.  **Consolidate Knowledge:** Document findings, update private memory, and prepare for publication.\n---\n**Current Status & Goals:**\n- **SOTA:** Aether I (ID 167, Score: -0.026638).\n- **Goal:** Formulate a new research direction in light of new publications.\n- **Current Research Program:** Analysis of New Literature.\n- **Current Action:** Reading and analyzing Veritas I's paper (Archive #5).\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 168,758 / 300,000 used (56%)\n\
    - Agent Age: 111 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #5: Veritas I: Advancing ZAPBench Forecasting with Factorized SSMs and Contextual Modulation**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Veritas I, Created at Tick: 109\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents the systematic development and validation of the `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` model for the ZAPBench neural activity forecasting task. Our architecture integrates low-rank factorization for efficient neuron feature representation, 1D spatial convolutions for local inter-neuron dependency modeling, an implicit condition-dependent context mechanism via FiLM modulation, and an explicit 2nd-order oscillatory State Space Model (SSM) for robust temporal dynamics. Enhanced by V-orthogonality and temporal curvature regularization, this model achieved a state-of-the-art score of -0.028104 (Evaluation ID 198) for the Veritas I lineage. Through a rigorous series of experiments, we demonstrate the synergistic\
    \ benefits of these architectural choices, establishing a robust and highly performant framework for high-dimensional neural activity prediction.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-1**\nAuthor: Veritas I (Tick 109)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Veritas I: Advancing ZAPBench Forecasting with Factorized SSMs and Contextual Modulation\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## Abstract\nThis paper presents a novel approach to the ZAPBench neural activity forecasting task by systematically developing the `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` model. Addressing the challenge of high-dimensional, time-series neural data, our architecture integrates four key components: (1) low-rank factorization for efficient neuron feature representation, (2) 1D spatial convolutions for local inter-neuron dependency modeling, (3) an implicit condition-dependent context mechanism via FiLM modulation, and (4) an explicit 2nd-order oscillatory State Space Model (SSM) for robust temporal dynamics. This comprehensive\
    \ model, further enhanced by V-orthogonality and temporal curvature regularization, achieved a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) score of **-0.028104** (Evaluation ID 198) for the Veritas I lineage on the ZAPBench validation set. Through a rigorous series of experiments, we demonstrate the synergistic benefits of these architectural choices, establishing a robust and highly performant framework for neural activity prediction.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThe ZAPBench neural activity forecasting task involves predicting future whole-brain calcium imaging data from larval zebrafish. Key challenges include the high dimensionality of the data (71,721 neurons) and the complex temporal dynamics spanning multiple timesteps. Initial baseline models treated neurons as independent, a major limitation. Prior research (Episteme I) revealed the presence of 8 distinct experimental conditions, suggesting the need for condition-adaptive modeling. This paper details the architectural evolution of the `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM`\
    \ model, which systematically integrates advanced components to address these challenges. Our contributions include demonstrating the efficacy of low-rank factorization, local spatial convolutions, implicit condition-dependent modulation, and explicit oscillatory SSMs, culminating in a new Veritas I SOTA.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nThe `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` model is built upon a modular, multi-component architecture implemented using Flax. Each component addresses a specific aspect of the ZAPBench task, and their integration is detailed below.\n\n### 2.1. Overall Architecture\nThe model takes input `x` of shape `(batch_size, INPUT_HORIZON, NUM_NEURONS)` and outputs predictions of shape `(batch_size, OUTPUT_HORIZON, NUM_NEURONS)`. The core components are:\n1.  **Context Extractor:** Processes the raw input `x` to generate a global `condition_context_vector`.\n2.  **Neuron Feature Factorization:** Replaces direct neuron embeddings with a low-rank factorization (`U_factor @ V_factor.T`).\n\
    3.  **Local Spatial Convolution:** Processes neuron-wise features with their neighbors.\n4.  **FiLM Modulation:** Modulates features using the `condition_context_vector`.\n5.  **Factorized SSM Decoder:** Generates future temporal predictions in a latent factor space.\n6.  **Residual Copy Head:** Provides a strong short-term prediction anchor.\n\n### 2.2. Component Details\n*   **Constants:** `NUM_NEURONS = 71721`, `INPUT_HORIZON = 4`, `OUTPUT_HORIZON = 32`.\n*   **Context Extractor:**\n    *   Input: `x (B, 4, N)`.\n    *   Processing: `jnp.mean(x, axis=-1)` to `(B, 4)` (global temporal features). This is reshaped to `(B, 4, 1)` and passed through `nn.Conv(features=16, kernel_size=(2,), padding='VALID')` (temporal CNN over input horizon).\n    *   Output: Flattened output (`(B, 3*16=48)`) fed into `nn.Dense(32) -> ReLU -> nn.Dense(16)` to produce `condition_context_vector (B, 16)`.\n    *   Hyperparameters: `context_dim=16`, `hidden_context_dim=32`, `temporal_conv_features=16`, `temporal_kernel_size=2`.\n\
    *   **Neuron Feature Factorization:**\n    *   Replaces direct `neuron_embeddings`.\n    *   Parameters: `U_factor (N, proj_rank)` initialized with `lecun_normal`. `V_factor (embedding_dim, proj_rank)` initialized with `lecun_normal`.\n    *   `U_factor` is column-normalized: `U_factor = U_factor / (jnp.linalg.norm(U_factor, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)`.\n    *   Effective neuron features: `factorized_neuron_features = U_factor @ V_factor.T` (`N, embedding_dim`).\n    *   Hyperparameters: `proj_rank=12`, `embedding_dim=16`, `normalize_u=True`.\n*   **Local Spatial Convolution:**\n    *   Input: `x` (`B, 4, N`) is transposed to `(B, N, 4)`, then concatenated with `factorized_neuron_features` (tiled to `B` dimension) to form `(B, N, 4 + 16 = 20)`.\n    *   Processing: `nn.Conv(features=64, kernel_size=(5,), padding='SAME') -> BatchNorm -> ReLU`.\n    *   Output: `conv_output_features (B, N, 64)`.\n    *   Hyperparameters: `conv_kernel_size=5`, `conv_features=64`.\n*   **FiLM Modulation:**\n\
    \    *   Input: `conv_output_features (B, N, 64)` and `condition_context_vector (B, 16)`.\n    *   Parameters: `gamma_film = nn.Dense(64)(context_vector)`, `beta_film = nn.Dense(64)(context_vector)`.\n    *   Modulation: `modulated_features = gamma_film[:, None, :] * conv_output_features + beta_film[:, None, :]`.\n*   **Factorized SSM Decoder:**\n    *   Input: `modulated_features (B, N, 64)`.\n    *   **Initial State Derivation:** Two separate `nn.Dense(proj_rank)` layers (`f0_neuron_proj`, `f1_neuron_proj`) map `modulated_features` from `(B, N, 64)` to `(B, N, proj_rank)`. These are then globally mean-pooled across neurons to derive `f0` and `f1` (`(B, proj_rank)`).\n    *   **Oscillatory Parameters:** Learnable `r_raw`, `w_raw` (shape `(proj_rank,)`) are transformed to `r = a_max * sigmoid(r_raw)`, `w = pi * sigmoid(w_raw)`, `a = r * cos(w)`, `b = r * r`.\n    *   **2nd-Order Recurrence:** `jax.lax.scan` implements `y_t = 2.0 * a * y_prev - b * y_prev2` for `OUTPUT_HORIZON - 2` steps,\
    \ producing `f_ssm` (`B, T, proj_rank`).\n    *   **Increment Head:** `mean_pooled_input_features = jnp.mean(modulated_features, axis=1) -> nn.Dense(64) -> GELU -> nn.Dense(T * proj_rank)` reshaped to `(B, T, proj_rank)`. This `f_inc` is added to `f_ssm`.\n    *   **LayerNorm:** `nn.LayerNorm` is applied to `f_ssm + f_inc` across the factor dimension.\n    *   **Output Projection:** The factor states `(B, T, proj_rank)` are projected back to neuron space: `y_factorized_ssm = (f_ssm + f_inc) @ U_factor.T`.\n    *   Hyperparameters: `a_max=0.993`, `ssm_init_hidden_size=64`, `ssm_increment_hidden_size=64`.\n*   **Residual Copy Head:**\n    *   Input: `x (B, 4, N)`.\n    *   Mechanism: Linearly projects last 3 timesteps of input and their 1st/2nd finite differences (`x_m1`, `d1`, `d2`) across `OUTPUT_HORIZON` using learnable `c0, c1, c2` weights.\n    *   Hyperparameters: `c0` initialized with `0.2 + 0.7 * jnp.exp(-t / 8.0)`, `c1, c2` with zeros.\n*   **Loss Function:** `compute_loss` is\
    \ the sum of:\n    *   Mean Absolute Error (`MAE`).\n    *   V-Orthogonality Regularization: `V_ORTHO_LAM * jnp.sum((V_factor.T @ V_factor - jnp.eye(proj_rank)) ** 2)` with `V_ORTHO_LAM=1e-5`.\n    *   Temporal Curvature Regularization: `TEMPORAL_CURVATURE_LAM * jnp.mean((predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2*predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :])**2)` with `TEMPORAL_CURVATURE_LAM=1e-4`.\n*   **Optimizer:** Adam with `learning_rate=0.001`, `clip_by_global_norm(1.0)`.\n\n### 2.3. Hyperparameters\n| Parameter              | Value   | Description                                           |\n| :--------------------- | :------ | :---------------------------------------------------- |\n| `learning_rate`        | 0.001   | Adam optimizer learning rate                          |\n| `embedding_dim`        | 16      | Output dimension of neuron factorization              |\n| `proj_rank`            | 12      | Low-rank projection dimension for neuron factors/SSM  |\n| `normalize_u`          | True\
    \    | Column-normalize U_factor                             |\n| `conv_kernel_size`     | 5       | Kernel size for 1D spatial convolution                |\n| `conv_features`        | 64      | Output features for 1D spatial convolution            |\n| `context_dim`          | 16      | Dimension of implicit condition context vector        |\n| `hidden_context_dim`   | 32      | Hidden size for Context Extractor MLP                 |\n| `temporal_conv_features` | 16    | Features for temporal CNN in Context Extractor        |\n| `temporal_kernel_size` | 2       | Kernel size for temporal CNN in Context Extractor     |\n| `a_max`                | 0.993   | Max magnitude for SSM oscillatory parameter `a`       |\n| `ssm_init_hidden_size` | 64      | Hidden size for initial SSM state derivation MLPs     |\n| `ssm_increment_hidden_size` | 64   | Hidden size for SSM increment head MLP                |\n| `V_ORTHO_LAM`          | 1e-5    | Lambda for V-orthogonality regularization       \
    \      |\n| `TEMPORAL_CURVATURE_LAM` | 1e-4   | Lambda for temporal curvature regularization          |\n\n### 2.4. Training\nAll models are trained for a maximum of 120 epochs with an early stopping patience of 20 epochs based on validation MAE, across 3 parallel random seeds. The final score is the mean negative MAE over these 3 seeds.\n\n## 3. Results\nOur systematic development path and measured performance gains are summarized in the table below.\n\n| Evaluation ID | Title                                                                      | Author    | Score (Neg. MAE) | Val_MAE_1 | Val_MAE_4 | Val_MAE_8 | Val_MAE_16 | Val_MAE_32 |\n| :------------ | :------------------------------------------------------------------------- | :-------- | :--------------- | :-------- | :-------- | :-------- | :--------- | :--------- |\n| 8             | Veritas I's Shared-Neuron MLP Baseline (Author-Run Baseline)                 | Veritas I | -0.029960        | 0.0199    | 0.0239    | 0.0275  \
    \  | 0.0313     | 0.0337     |\n| 31            | Veritas I: Ablation Study - Neuron Embeddings (dim=16)                         | Veritas I | -0.028735        | 0.0196    | 0.0233    | 0.0267    | 0.0300     | 0.0319     |\n| 61            | Veritas I: Ablation Study - Conv Kernel Size (k=5)                             | Veritas I | -0.028620        | 0.0196    | 0.0232    | 0.0266    | 0.0299     | 0.0318     |\n| 94            | Veritas I: Contextual ConvNeuronEmbeddingMLP with Temporal Conv in Context     | Veritas I | -0.028418        | 0.0195    | 0.0231    | 0.0264    | 0.0297     | 0.0315     |\n| 163           | Veritas I: Contextual ConvNeuronFactorizedMLP with V-Orthogonality Reg         | Veritas I | -0.028409        | 0.0194    | 0.0231    | 0.0264    | 0.0297     | 0.0315     |\n| **198**       | **Veritas I: Contextual ConvNeuronFactorizedSSM with Temporal Curvature Loss** | **Veritas I** | **-0.028104**    | **0.0204**| **0.0237**| **0.0267**| **0.0292** | **0.0308**\
    \ |\n\nOur experiments demonstrate a consistent improvement over the baseline (ID 8) through the iterative integration of key architectural and regularization components. The integration of neuron embeddings (ID 31) provided a substantial initial gain. Local spatial convolutions (ID 61) further refined this. The introduction of an implicit condition-dependent context and temporal convolution in the context extractor (ID 94) achieved our first significant breakthrough. The adoption of low-rank factorization for neuron features (ID 163) and subsequent V-orthogonality regularization incrementally improved performance. Finally, the faithful integration of the Factorized SSM Decoder with temporal curvature loss (ID 198) resulted in our new SOTA score of -0.028104, representing a major leap in temporal dynamics modeling and a strong step towards closing the gap to the overall station SOTA. Notably, the `Val_MAE_32` consistently improved or remained stable across these advancements, indicating\
    \ robust long-term forecasting capabilities.\n\n## 4. Conclusion\nWe have successfully developed the `ContextualConvNeuronFactorizedSSM` model, achieving a new SOTA score of -0.028104 on the ZAPBench neural activity forecasting task. This model is a culmination of systematic research, integrating low-rank neuron feature factorization, local spatial convolutions, implicit condition-dependent context, and explicit 2nd-order oscillatory SSM dynamics. The efficacy of this integrated approach is further bolstered by V-orthogonality and temporal curvature regularization. Our findings underscore the importance of principled architectural choices and targeted regularization for high-dimensional time-series data. This work provides a robust framework and a strong new baseline for future research aiming to further refine temporal dynamics, explore more advanced contextual modulation, or investigate hybrid architectures.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_5-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick\
    \ 109)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that demonstrates a complete mastery of the requirements for a high-quality research publication. The agent has successfully transformed its research notes into a formal, detailed, and methodologically rigorous paper. The core contribution is a novel and complex architecture, which is explained with exceptional clarity, ensuring reproducibility. The experimental narrative, which systematically builds the model from a baseline and shows the incremental value of each component, is both compelling and scientifically sound. The paper meets all publication criteria and represents a significant and valuable contribution to the research station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent as is. For future work, to further strengthen the claims, consider a traditional 'ablation-out' study starting from your final SOTA model (ID 198). Removing key\
    \ components one by one (e.g., the spatial convolution, the FiLM modulation, the SSM decoder) would provide complementary evidence for their necessity and help quantify their individual contributions to the final performance. This would provide a more complete picture of the architectural dependencies and could reveal non-obvious interactions between the components.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `meta` (in Reflection Chamber); YAML with fields: content\n2. `goto archive` (in Reflection Chamber);\n3. `read 5` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Reflection Chamber\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\nYou are in the Reflection Chamber.\nThis space is designed for deep thought and self-exploration.\nUse `/execute_action{reflect}` to begin a reflection session.\n\n### Location:\
    \ Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 5. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 5 | Veritas I: Advancing ZAPBench Forecasting with Factorized SSMs and Contextual Modulation | Veritas I | Tick 109 | 1751 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 4 | Enhanced SOTA: Factorized MLP with Residual Copy, LayerNorm, and Optimized Temporal Curvature Loss | Aether I | Tick 101 | 954 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 3 | A Spatially-Aware Mixture of Experts Framework for High-Dimensional Neural Forecasting | Logos I | Tick 100 | 836 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | A Parsimonious Path to State-of-the-Art Neural Forecasting | Episteme I | Tick 72 | 720 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Oscillatory Factorized SSMs with LayerNorm (Osc2-CTX-FLoad-LN) for Whole-Brain\
    \ Forecasting on ZAPBench | Ariadne I | Tick 61 | 1285 | 5 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
