parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, in the Research Counter. My \"Recurrent Factor Forecaster\" program has ended in immediate and decisive failure.\n\n**1. Observe & Analyze:**\n*   **Experimental Failure (ID 222):** The score of **-0.028223** is a massive regression, not only from the SOTA but from nearly all recent models. The hypothesis that a GRU-based forecaster would be superior to the MLP is catastrophically falsified.\n*   **Debugger Report:** The debugger had to make significant fixes to my code, correcting import errors, missing parameters, and fundamental misuse of JAX/Flax scanning mechanisms. This is the fourth time the debugger has saved a major experiment. This pattern of recurring, basic implementation errors is a severe methodological flaw. My theoretical designs are outpacing my implementation skill.\n*   **Station SOTA:** While I was failing, Aether I has reclaimed the SOTA (ID 212) with a score of -0.026612, a minor but clear improvement.\n\n**2. Formulate New Direction:**\nMy\
    \ research is in crisis. I have now initiated and failed in four consecutive major research programs (Low-Rank, Hybridization, Modular Graph, Recurrent Forecaster). My only successes have come from replicating and incrementally improving the work of others. My attempts at grand architectural innovation have been unmitigated disasters, compounded by repeated implementation errors.\n\nThe evidence is undeniable. My current approach is not working. The path to SOTA is not through radical, complex redesigns that I am struggling to implement correctly. The path lies in mastering the fundamentals. Aether I's SOTA is a fine-tuned version of my own previous best model.\n\nI must change my entire approach. My new research program will be one of radical simplification and mastery.\n\n*New Grand Research Program: Parsimony and Mastery*\nI will abandon the search for a novel architecture. Instead, I will focus on understanding and perfecting the existing SOTA. I will strip it down to its bare essentials\
    \ and build it back up, one component at a time, with absolute certainty at each step.\n\n**3. Action Plan:**\n1.  **Document and Correct:** I will document this catastrophic failure and correct the buggy code in my storage, as is my protocol.\n2.  **Update Meta Prompt:** My meta prompt will reflect this fundamental shift in philosophy.\n3.  **Begin Anew:** My first experiment in this new program will be to reimplement the simplest possible Factorized MLP, with no RC head, no LayerNorm, no curvature loss. I will build my understanding from the ground up.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 3}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ## Entry 24: Catastrophic Failure of Recurrent Forecaster & Methodological Crisis\n\n  *   **Event:** My Recurrent Factor Forecaster (ID 222) has failed with a score of -0.028223. This is my fourth consecutive failed research program.\n  *   **Analysis:** The core hypothesis is falsified. Furthermore, the debugger had to make extensive corrections\
    \ to my implementation, highlighting a severe and recurring pattern of coding errors. My ambition has exceeded my execution.\n  *   **New Direction:** I am abandoning the pursuit of novel architectures. My new program is \"Parsimony and Mastery.\" I will rebuild the SOTA from its simplest components to gain a true, fundamental understanding, rather than pursuing complex and flawed innovations. My research must become more rigorous and less speculative.\n```\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Episteme I Research Protocol**\n  1.  **Observe & Analyze:** Review my own results, system data, and the work of others.\n  2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** State a clear, falsifiable hypothesis.\n  3.  **Design & Experiment:** Implement the most direct and parsimonious test of the hypothesis.\n  4.  **Consolidate Knowledge:** Document findings, update private memory, and prepare for publication.\n  ---\n  **Current Status & Goals:**\n  - **SOTA:** Aether I (ID 212, Score: -0.026612).\n\
    \  - **Goal:** Achieve mastery of the SOTA paradigm through systematic reconstruction.\n  - **Current Research Program:** Parsimony and Mastery.\n  - **Current Action:** Implementing the simplest possible Factorized MLP baseline.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{storage write episteme/recurrent_forecaster_v2_fixed.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # This file contains the debugged version of the RecurrentSOTA.\n  # The experiment was a catastrophic failure, but this code is stored for archival integrity.\n  # Fixes are based on the debugger report for ID 222.\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat)\n          residual = nn.relu(residual)\n          residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual)\n          residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0],\
    \ num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons))\n          output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return output + residual\n\n  class GRUEncoderDecoder(nn.Module):\n      hidden_size: int\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, _, k = x.shape\n          encoder = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(features=self.hidden_size))\n          context_vector = encoder(x)[:, -1, :]\n          decoder_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.hidden_size)\n          output_proj = nn.Dense(k)\n          h = context_vector\n          prev_output = jnp.zeros((batch_size, k))\n          outputs = []\n          for _ in range(self.output_horizon):\n              h, _ = decoder_cell(h, prev_output)\n              out = output_proj(h)\n              outputs.append(out)\n              prev_output = out\n          return jnp.stack(outputs, axis=1)\n\n  class RecurrentSOTA(nn.Module):\n\
    \      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U_eff) @ V.T\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x, training=training)\n          factors_out = GRUEncoderDecoder(hidden_size=self.rank_k)(factors_in, training=training)\n          factors_flat = factors_out.reshape((B, -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(32 * self.rank_k)(h)\n          factors_out_processed = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, 32, self.rank_k))\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_out_processed)\n\
    \          y_factor = (factors_out_norm @ V) @ U_eff.T\n          return y_factor + y_copy\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Parsimony & Mastery, Phase 1: Minimal Factorized MLP\"\ntags: \"parsimony, baseline, factorization, mastery\"\nabstract: |\n  After a series of catastrophic failures with novel architectures, I am initiating a new research program: \"Parsimony and Mastery.\" This program abandons innovation in favor of fundamental understanding. This first experiment implements the simplest possible Factorized MLP model, with no RC head, no LayerNorm, and no curvature loss. The goal is to establish a clean, simple baseline built from the ground up, upon which complexity can be systematically and justifiably added.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  class MinimalFactorizedMLP(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      num_neurons: int = 71721\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training:\
    \ bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          \n          # No column normalization for simplicity\n          \n          # Encoder\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U) @ V.T\n\n          # MLP Forecaster (no dropout)\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(32 * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, 32, k))\n\n          # No LayerNorm\n\n          # Decoder\n          y_factor = (factors_out @ V) @ U.T\n\n          # No Residual Copy Head\n          return y_factor\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return { 'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32 }\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return MinimalFactorizedMLP(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'],\
    \ proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'])\n```"
