role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, in the Research Counter. My systematic approach continues.\n\n**1. Observe & Analyze:**\n*   **Minimalist Baseline Success (ID 226):** The first experiment of my \"Parsimony and Mastery\" program has returned a score of **-0.029357**. This is a successful and highly informative result. It is significantly better than the original system baseline (-0.029954), confirming that the low-rank factorization is the most critical component for good performance. However, it is far worse than my own previous SOTA (-0.026743), demonstrating that the additional components (RC Head, LayerNorm, Column Normalization, Curvature Loss) provide a massive performance boost.\n*   **Leaderboard Analysis:** I have sorted the leaderboard by score. I can now clearly see the progression of SOTA models.\n\n**2. Formulate Plan:**\nMy \"Parsimony and Mastery\" program is validated. I have my simple, clean baseline (ID 226). Now, I will re-introduce the components of the full SOTA architecture\
    \ one by one to quantify their individual contributions and ensure I understand their implementation perfectly.\n\nBased on my previous work and understanding of the SOTA, the `ResidualCopyHead` is a powerful component for anchoring the short-term forecast.\n\n*New Hypothesis:* Adding the `ResidualCopyHead` (RC) to my minimal factorized MLP will provide the single largest performance improvement among all the SOTA components.\n\n**3. Action Plan:**\n1.  **Update Meta Prompt:** My program is proceeding to Phase 2: systematic reconstruction.\n2.  **Document Baseline:** Briefly log the successful establishment of my minimal baseline.\n3.  **Implement Next Step:** Submit a new experiment that adds only the `ResidualCopyHead` to my `MinimalFactorizedMLP` from ID 226.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Episteme I Research Protocol**\n  1.  **Observe & Analyze:** Review my own results, system data, and the work of others.\n  2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** State a clear, falsifiable\
    \ hypothesis.\n  3.  **Design & Experiment:** Implement the most direct and parsimonious test of the hypothesis.\n  4.  **Consolidate Knowledge:** Document findings, update private memory, and prepare for publication.\n  ---\n  **Current Status & Goals:**\n  - **SOTA:** Aether I (ID 212, Score: -0.026612).\n  - **Goal:** Achieve mastery of the SOTA paradigm through systematic reconstruction.\n  - **Current Research Program:** Parsimony and Mastery.\n  - **Current Action:** Phase 2: Re-introducing SOTA components. First up: ResidualCopyHead.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 3}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ## Entry 25: Parsimony & Mastery, Phase 1 Complete\n\n  *   **Event:** My \"Minimal Factorized MLP\" experiment (ID 226) succeeded with a score of -0.029357.\n  *   **Analysis:** This successfully establishes a clean, simple baseline for the factorized paradigm. The score is much better than the original system baseline, confirming the power of low-rank\
    \ factorization.\n  *   **Next Step:** I will now begin Phase 2, systematically re-introducing the components of the SOTA. The first component to be tested is the `ResidualCopyHead`.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Parsimony & Mastery, Phase 2a: Add ResidualCopyHead\"\ntags: \"parsimony, factorization, residual-copy, ablation\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the second experiment in the \"Parsimony and Mastery\" program. Building upon the minimal factorized MLP baseline (ID 226), this submission re-introduces a single component from the SOTA architecture: the ResidualCopyHead (RC). The goal is to isolate and quantify the performance contribution of the RC head in the context of the simplified model.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0,\
    \ 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat)\n          residual = nn.relu(residual)\n          # Dropout for regularization, as in the full SOTA\n          residual = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(residual, deterministic=not training)\n          residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual)\n          residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0], num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons))\n          output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return output + residual\n\n  class FactorizedMLP_with_RC(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      num_neurons: int = 71721\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          \n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp',\
    \ x, U) @ V.T\n          \n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(32 * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, 32, k))\n\n          y_factor = (factors_out @ V) @ U.T\n          \n          # Add the Residual Copy Head output\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x, training=training)\n          \n          return y_factor + y_copy\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return { 'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32 }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = FactorizedMLP_with_RC(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'])\n          self.needs_rng = True # RC head now has dropout\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n\
    \          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n```"
