role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, in the Archive Room. My second publication attempt has been rejected.\n\n**1. Observe & Analyze:**\nThis failure is different from the first. My first paper was rejected for a lack of detail and rigor. This one was rejected for failing to meet specific, rule-based experimental criteria outlined in the Codex, which I had overlooked. The core *idea* of the paper was praised. The failure is procedural, not scientific. The reviewer's feedback is, once again, precise and actionable.\n\n**Failures:**\n1.  **Missing Experiment:** I need a minimum of five experiments. My study had four.\n2.  **Missing Baseline:** I did not run the standard system baseline myself.\n3.  **Missing Generalization:** My key finding (the power of normalization) was not tested in a second context.\n\n**2. Formulate Plan:**\nThe reviewer's suggestions provide a perfect, efficient path to resolving all three issues simultaneously.\n*   **Action A (Solves 1 & 2):** I will run the standard system\
    \ baseline. This will be my fifth experiment and satisfy the baseline comparison rule. I have already done this once (ID 86), but I will do it again to ensure the control is contemporary with my other experiments.\n*   **Action B (Solves 3):** I will test my main finding—the power of normalization—on a different architecture. The reviewer suggests applying it to the system baseline. This is an excellent idea. It will be my sixth experiment and will satisfy the generalization requirement.\n\n**3. Action Plan:**\nMy course is clear. I will immediately execute the two required experiments. Since they are independent, I can submit them in the same turn.\n\n1.  **Update Meta Prompt:** The goal is now explicitly to address the reviewer's feedback.\n2.  **Go to Research Counter:** The required location for submitting experiments.\n3.  **Submit Experiment A:** Re-run the system baseline.\n4.  **Submit Experiment B:** Run the system baseline *with* my normalization techniques added.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n\
    ```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Episteme I Research Protocol**\n  1.  **Observe & Analyze:** Review my own results, system data, and the work of others.\n  2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** State a clear, falsifiable hypothesis.\n  3.  **Design & Experiment:** Implement the most direct and parsimonious test of the hypothesis.\n  4.  **Consolidate Knowledge:** Document findings, update private memory, and prepare for publication.\n  ---\n  **Current Status & Goals:**\n  - **SOTA:** Aether I (ID 212, Score: -0.026612).\n  - **Goal:** Fulfill reviewer requirements to publish my \"Parsimony and Mastery\" paper.\n  - **Current Research Program:** Publication Validation.\n  - **Current Action:** Running two required experiments: an author-run baseline and a generalization test.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Publication Validation: Author-Run System Baseline\"\ntags: \"publication, baseline, control, replication\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment\
    \ is one of two runs required to address reviewer feedback on a rejected paper. It is a straightforward re-run of the standard system baseline (Shared-Neuron MLP). This serves to fulfill the five-experiment minimum and the requirement for an author-run baseline for comparison.\ncontent: |\n  # This submission uses the system defaults for all functions.\n  # No code is necessary.\n```\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Publication Validation: Generalization Test on System Baseline\"\ntags: \"publication, generalization, ablation, normalization, layernorm\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment is the second of two runs required to address reviewer feedback. It tests the generalization of my key finding from the \"Parsimony and Mastery\" program—the power of normalization. I apply LayerNorm to the output of the standard system baseline (Shared-Neuron MLP) to determine if this technique's benefits are specific to the factorized architecture or if they apply more broadly.\ncontent:\
    \ |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  class NormalizedSharedNeuronMLP(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      The default system baseline, but with LayerNorm applied to the final output\n      before it is returned.\n      \"\"\"\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons = x.shape\n          x = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          x = jnp.reshape(x, (batch_size * num_neurons, input_horizon))\n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(x)\n          x = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(x)\n          x = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(x)\n          x = jnp.reshape(x, (batch_size, num_neurons, self.output_horizon))\n          x = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          \n     \
    \     # Generalization test: Apply LayerNorm\n          x = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          \n          return x\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self):\n          self.model = NormalizedSharedNeuronMLP()\n          self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n          self.needs_rng = True\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = jax.random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout}, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if mutable:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs)\n              return output, updates\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper()\n```"
