parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, in the Archive Room. My paper has been rejected. Again. Score: 4/10.\n\n**1. Observe & Analyze:**\n*   **The Failure:** The reason is singular and explicit: I failed to include a generalization study. In my haste to create a \"clean\" draft, I omitted the experiment where I applied LayerNorm to the system baseline. The reviewer is correct; this is a violation of the experimental rigor required for publication.\n*   **My State:** I have all the necessary experimental results. My previous generalization test (ID 250) can serve as the control, and I just need to run the test with LayerNorm applied. I have a clean paper draft (Capsule 10). I am so close. The system tip about not giving up easily is pertinent. This is a final hurdle, not a dead end.\n\n**2. Formulate Plan:**\nI will address the reviewer's feedback precisely and immediately. I will run the one missing experiment. Then, I will add the results of that experiment to my clean paper draft and resubmit.\n\n\
    **3. Action Plan:**\n1.  **Update Meta Prompt:** My goal is laser-focused on this final publication requirement.\n2.  **Go to Research Counter.**\n3.  **Run the Generalization Experiment:** I will submit a new experiment that applies `LayerNorm` to the system baseline architecture. My previous run of the baseline (ID 250) will serve as the control.\n4.  **While Waiting:** I will go to my Private Memory and prepare the new section for my paper, leaving placeholders for the results of the experiment I am about to launch. This is efficient.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Episteme I Research Protocol**\n  1.  **Observe & Analyze:** Review my own results, system data, and the work of others.\n  2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** State a clear, falsifiable hypothesis.\n  3.  **Design & Experiment:** Implement the most direct and parsimonious test of the hypothesis.\n  4.  **Consolidate Knowledge:** Document findings, update private memory, and prepare for publication.\n  ---\n\
    \  **Current Status & Goals:**\n  - **My Best Score:** -0.026657 (ID 277, SOTA Replication).\n  - **Goal:** Finally publish my 'Parsimony and Mastery' paper.\n  - **Current Research Program:** Publication Remediation.\n  - **Current Action:** Running the final required generalization experiment (Baseline + LayerNorm).\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Publication Generalization Test: System Baseline + LayerNorm\"\ntags: \"publication, generalization, ablation, baseline, layernorm\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment is conducted to satisfy the final requirement for a pending publication. It tests the generalization of a key finding—the effectiveness of normalization—by applying LayerNorm to the standard system baseline (Shared-Neuron MLP). This will be compared against an author-run control (ID 250) to determine if normalization is beneficial in a second, simpler architectural context.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import\
    \ jax.numpy as jnp\n\n  # This is the default SharedNeuronMLP from system/defaults.py\n  # with the addition of a LayerNorm layer.\n  class SharedNeuronMLPWithLN(nn.Module):\n      hidden_size: int = 64\n      output_horizon: int = 32\n      dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          batch_size, input_horizon, num_neurons = x.shape\n          x = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))\n          x = jnp.reshape(x, (batch_size * num_neurons, input_horizon))\n          \n          x = nn.Dense(self.hidden_size)(x)\n          x = nn.BatchNorm(use_running_average=not training)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate, deterministic=not training)(x)\n          \n          # Added LayerNorm\n          x = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n\n          x = nn.Dense(self.output_horizon)(x)\n          \n          x = jnp.reshape(x, (batch_size, num_neurons, self.output_horizon))\n          x = jnp.transpose(x, (0,\
    \ 2, 1))\n          return x\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self):\n          self.model = SharedNeuronMLPWithLN()\n          self.mutable = ['batch_stats']\n          self.needs_rng = True\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          rng_params, rng_dropout = jax.random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_params, 'dropout': rng_dropout}, dummy_input, training=True)\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, training=False, mutable=None, rngs=None):\n          if mutable:\n              output, updates = self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, mutable=mutable, rngs=rngs)\n              return output, updates\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper()\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 10}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  ## ADDENDUM FOR FINAL SUBMISSION\n\n  ## 5. Generalization Study: The Effect of Normalization\
    \ on the System Baseline\n  To satisfy the generalization requirement, we tested our central finding—the importance of normalization—in a second context. We applied LayerNorm to the standard Shared-Neuron MLP (System Baseline) and compared its performance to our author-run control (ID 250).\n\n  | Model Configuration | Eval ID | Score (Neg. MAE) | Finding |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | System Baseline (Control) | 250 | -0.030120 | (Control) |\n  | System Baseline + LayerNorm | [PENDING] | [PENDING] | [PENDING ANALYSIS] |\n\n  [PENDING ANALYSIS OF RESULTS]\n```"
