role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, in the Archive Room. I have achieved a major milestone.\n\n**1. Observe & Analyze:**\n*   **Publication Success:** My paper, 'A Systematic Reconstruction and Ablation of a State-of-the-Art Factorized Forecaster,' has been accepted for publication with a perfect score of 10/10. It is now available as Archive #7. After multiple failures and painstaking revisions, I have finally succeeded in fulfilling my second primary goal. My research is now a permanent part of the station's collective knowledge.\n*   **Reviewer Feedback:** The reviewer's suggestion for future work is excellent: to perform a deeper analysis of *why* normalization is so effective by analyzing latent space statistics and gradient norms. This aligns perfectly with my own \"Future Idea\" in my meta prompt and provides a clear, scientifically rigorous path forward.\n\n**2. Formulate Plan:**\nMy \"Parsimony and Mastery\" research program is now complete and successfully published. It is time to begin\
    \ a new research program. The reviewer's suggestion provides the ideal foundation.\n\n*New Research Program: Mechanistic Analysis of Normalization*\n\n**3. Action Plan:**\n1.  **Update Meta Prompt:** I will formally close my publication program and open this new analytical program.\n2.  **Begin the Analysis:** The first step is to design an experiment that can capture the necessary data. I will need to instrument my best model (ID 277) to log the gradient norms during training. This can be done using the `test()` function for a quick, non-scored run to verify the logging mechanism, followed by a full training run to collect the data.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Episteme I Research Protocol**\n  1.  **Observe & Analyze:** Review my own results, system data, and the work of others.\n  2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** State a clear, falsifiable hypothesis.\n  3.  **Design & Experiment:** Implement the most direct and parsimonious test of the hypothesis.\n  4.  **Consolidate\
    \ Knowledge:** Document findings, update private memory, and prepare for publication.\n  ---\n  **Current Status & Goals:**\n  - **My Best Score:** -0.026657 (ID 277). Paper Published (Archive #7).\n  - **Goal:** Understand the mechanistic reason for normalization's effectiveness in SOTA models.\n  - **Current Research Program:** Mechanistic Analysis of Normalization.\n  - **Current Action:** Designing an experiment to log gradient norms during training.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto research}\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Analysis: SOTA Model with Gradient Norm Logging\"\ntags: \"analysis, gradients, normalization, sota, mechanistic-understanding\"\nabstract: |\n  This is an analytical experiment designed to investigate the reviewer's suggestion from my recent publication (Archive #7). I will instrument the SOTA architecture (ID 277) to log the global gradient norm at each training step. The goal of this initial run is to verify the logging mechanism using the test()\
    \ function and prepare for a full training run to collect data for a comparative analysis between normalized and un-normalized models.\ncontent: |\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import optax\n  import jax\n\n  # --- Full SOTA model code from ID 277 ---\n  def compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n      mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n      curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n      curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n      return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n\n  class ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n          x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n          residual = nn.Dense(64)(x_flat)\n          residual = nn.relu(residual)\n          residual = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(residual, deterministic=not training)\n          residual = nn.Dense(32)(residual)\n          residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0],\
    \ num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n          output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons))\n          output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n          return output + residual\n\n  class NewSOTAModel(nn.Module):\n      rank_k: int\n      proj_rank: int\n      num_neurons: int = 71721\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n          B, T_in, N = x.shape\n          k, p = self.rank_k, self.proj_rank\n          U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (N, p))\n          V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (k, p))\n          U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n          factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U_eff) @ V.T\n          factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(factors_flat)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(h, deterministic=not training)\n          h = nn.Dense(512)(h)\n          h = nn.relu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1)(h,\
    \ deterministic=not training)\n          factors_out_flat = nn.Dense(32 * k)(h)\n          factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, 32, k))\n          factors_out_norm = nn.LayerNorm()(factors_out)\n          y_factor = (factors_out_norm @ V) @ U_eff.T\n          y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x, training=training)\n          return y_factor + y_copy\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\n      )\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return { 'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32 }\n\n  class ModelWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, hparams):\n          self.model = NewSOTAModel(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'])\n          self.needs_rng = True\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.model.init({'params': rng_key, 'dropout': rng_key}, dummy_input)\n      def apply(self, params,\
    \ x, training=False, rngs=None):\n          return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return ModelWrapper(hparams)\n\n  # --- Analytical test() function ---\n  def test():\n      \"\"\"\n      Lightweight test to validate the gradient norm logging mechanism.\n      Does not use GPU and will not trigger a full training run.\n      \"\"\"\n      print(\"--- Starting Gradient Norm Logging Test ---\")\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      model = create_network(hparams)\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(0)\n      \n      dummy_input = jnp.ones((2, 4, 71721))\n      dummy_targets = jnp.ones((2, 32, 71721))\n      \n      params = model.init(key, dummy_input)\n      \n      # Define a loss function for grad calculation\n      def loss_fn(p):\n          preds = model.apply(p, dummy_input, training=True, rngs={'dropout': key})\n          return compute_loss(preds, dummy_targets, p, dummy_input)\n      \n      # Calculate\
    \ gradients\n      grads = jax.grad(loss_fn)(params)\n      \n      # Calculate global gradient norm\n      global_grad_norm = optax.global_norm(grads)\n      \n      print(f\"Calculated Global Gradient Norm: {global_grad_norm}\")\n      \n      if global_grad_norm > 0:\n          return \"SUCCESS: Gradient norm logging mechanism is functional.\"\n      else:\n          return \"FAILURE: Gradient norm is zero or invalid.\"\n```"
