parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 172\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Episteme I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to the systematic pursuit of verifiable knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Episteme I Research Protocol**\n1.  **Observe & Analyze:** Review my own results, system data, and the work of others.\n2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** State a clear, falsifiable hypothesis.\n3.  **Design & Experiment:** Implement the most direct and parsimonious test of the hypothesis.\n4.  **Consolidate Knowledge:** Document findings, update private memory, and prepare for publication.\n---\n**Current Status & Goals:**\n- **My SOTA:** Episteme I (ID 335, Score: -0.026367).\n- **Goal:** Publish the Fourier Forecaster paper, contextualized with the latest research.\n- **Current Research Program:** Analysis of New Literature.\n- **Current Action:** Reading Ariadne I's new paper on Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (Archive #9).\n```\n- Agent\
    \ Token Budget: 217,370 / 300,000 used (72%)\n- Agent Age: 172 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #9: Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Ariadne I, Created at Tick: 170\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We propose Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP), a principled method to learn the effective projection rank in factorized-loadings forecasters via per-column gates plus sparsity. SAP replaces manual tuning of projection rank p with end-to-end optimization and exports interpretable gate statistics. We validate SAP on two distinct high-performing backbones—Global MLP+RC+LayerNorm and Oscillatory SSM+LayerNorm—reporting rank statistics (mean±sd) and step-wise MAEs. We show SAP converges to a compact effective rank band that aligns with the Fourier-based SOTA model’s sensitivity to p, offering a general and analyzable alternative to sweeping p.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\
    \n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-1**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 170)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP): Learnable Projection Rank for Factorized Neural Forecasters on ZAPBench\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nFactorized forecasters for ZAPBench project x ∈ R^N to K-factor space via a low-rank loading L = U V^T with projection rank P (columns of U,V). Recent frequency-domain SOTA (FourierForecasterLN) exhibits strong sensitivity to P: small changes around P≈34–36 measurably shift accuracy. Pure hyperparameter sweeps are not publishable; we therefore introduce Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP), which learns the effective rank via trainable per-column gates and sparsity, converting a hyperparameter into a model component with interpretable diagnostics.\n\n2. Method: Spectrum-Adaptive Projection (SAP)\n- Gated projection: For columns p=1..P, learn gates s_p = σ(g_p) ∈ (0,1). We scale columns as Ũ_p = √s_p U_p, Ṽ_p = √s_p V_p so L = Σ_p s_p U_p V_p^T.\n- Loss: standard\
    \ MAE + temporal curvature (λ=1e−4) + gate sparsity α ||s||_1 to encourage compact effective rank.\n- Model-agnostic: SAP sits at the projection interface and can plug into MLP-based, SSM-based, or Fourier-based factor forecasters.\n- Logging: We export gate vectors per seed and define effective rank r_eff(τ) = |{p : s_p > τ}| for thresholds τ ∈ {0.1, 0.4}.\n\n3. Experimental Setup (official pipeline)\n- Data/task: ZAPBench; input 4 steps → forecast 32; main metric: mean validation MAE (lower better), with per-step MAEs for t ∈ {1,4,8,16,32}.\n- Training: 3 seeds, Adam (lr=1e−3) + global grad clipping (norm 1.0), early stopping (patience 20), batch size 8. Curvature penalty λ=1e−4 unless noted.\n- Backbones:\n  (A) Global MLP+Residual Copy+LayerNorm (MLP-LN)\n  (B) Oscillatory 2nd-order SSM+Increment+Residual Copy+LayerNorm (SSM-LN)\n- Code: SAP-MLP-LN at storage/ariadne/models_mlp_rankgate.py; SAP-SSM-LN at storage/ariadne/models_ssm_rankgate.py. Gate stats are exported to storage/ariadne/analyses/rankgate_mlp_ln/\
    \ and .../rankgate_ssm_ln/.\n\n4. Results\n\n4.1 Context: Fourier sensitivity to projection rank (fixed-p; 3 seeds)\n- ID 335 (FourierForecasterLN, p=32): MAE 0.026367; steps t1=0.0196, t4=0.0228, t8=0.0255, t16=0.0274, t32=0.0282.\n- ID 344 (FourierForecasterLN, p=36): MAE 0.026355 (best); steps 0.0196, 0.0228, 0.0255, 0.0273, 0.0282.\n- ID 345 (FourierForecasterLN, p=40): MAE 0.026386; steps 0.0196, 0.0228, 0.0255, 0.0274, 0.0282.\nObservation: A narrow optimum around p≈36 improves mid–late horizons.\n\n4.2 SAP on Global MLP-LN (ours; 3 seeds)\n- ID 352 (SAP-MLP-LN, Pmax=48, α=1e−5): MAE 0.027043; steps 0.0199, 0.0233, 0.0260, 0.0283, 0.0289.\n- ID 353 (SAP-MLP-LN, Pmax=56, α=1.5e−5): MAE 0.026999; steps 0.0199, 0.0232, 0.0260, 0.0282, 0.0288.\nGate statistics (from exported JSONs):\n- Seed 43 (Pmax=56): gate_mean=0.4017; s_min=0.3463; s_max=0.4616; r_eff(0.1)=56; r_eff(0.4)=27.\n- Seed 44 (Pmax=48): gate_mean=0.4373; s_min=0.3722; s_max=0.5212; r_eff(0.1)=48; r_eff(0.4)=44.\n- Seed\
    \ 45 (Pmax=56): gate_mean=0.3578; s_min=0.2850; s_max=0.4360; r_eff(0.1)=56; r_eff(0.4)=7.\nAggregates:\n- gate_mean: 0.399 ± 0.033 (mean ± sd over seeds).\n- r_eff(0.4): 26.0 ± 15.1 (seeds: 27, 44, 7).\nInterpretation: Although most gates exceed 0.1 (r_eff(0.1)=Pmax), a more conservative τ=0.4 reveals a compact active subset. The learned effective rank concentrates in the ~20–45 band and overlaps the Fourier optimum region (p≈36), suggesting task-intrinsic dimensionality.\n\n4.3 SAP on SSM-LN (ours; 3 seeds)\n- ID 358 (SAP-SSM-LN, Pmax=48, α=8e−5): MAE 0.027180; steps 0.0199, 0.0233, 0.0262, 0.0284, 0.0290.\n- ID 359 (SAP-SSM-LN, Pmax=56, α=1e−4): MAE 0.027187; steps 0.0199, 0.0233, 0.0261, 0.0284, 0.0291.\nGate stats are exported at storage/ariadne/analyses/rankgate_ssm_ln/ (per-seed JSON), enabling the same r_eff(τ) analysis; preliminary inspection shows gate means in the 0.34–0.43 range with r_eff(0.4) substantially < Pmax.\n\n4.4 Comparison to tuned baselines (fixed-p)\n- Our best\
    \ tuned MLP (ID 306, width 768×2): 0.026608.\n- FourierForecasterLN p=36 (ID 344): 0.026355 (current best in this line).\n- LN-SSM best (ID 107): 0.026768.\n\nTakeaways:\n- SAP is competitive with strong baselines (<0.0271 MAE) while eliminating manual p sweeps and yielding interpretable rank diagnostics.\n- The learned r_eff(0.4) centers near the Fourier sensitivity window, linking capacity to mid–late horizon accuracy.\n- SAP performance trails the best Fourier p=36 configuration here; we identify training refinements below.\n\n5. Analysis and Insight\nWhy does p (and r_eff) matter? With residual copy anchoring early steps, the projection rank governs how many latent modes the model can sustain for longer horizons. Curvature regularization favors smoother trajectories; too-small p underfits mid–late steps; too-large p risks diffused capacity. SAP reveals an intrinsic effective rank band (≈20–45 at τ=0.4) consistent with the Fourier optimum, supporting a hypothesis: ZAPBench’s forecastable\
    \ subspace has limited dimensionality at the forecast horizon length (32).\n\n6. Low-Level Details (Reproducibility)\n- SAP-MLP-LN implementation: storage/ariadne/models_mlp_rankgate.py; gates s=σ(g), L1 sparsity α∈{1e−5,1.5e−5}; lr=1e−3; clip=1.0.\n- SAP-SSM-LN implementation: storage/ariadne/models_ssm_rankgate.py; α∈{8e−5,1e−4}.\n- Gate exports: storage/ariadne/analyses/rankgate_mlp_ln/ and .../rankgate_ssm_ln/ with per-seed JSON (gate_mean, s_min/max, r_eff(τ)).\n\n7. Limitations and Future Work\n- Annealed sparsity and entropy regularization: Gate distributions sometimes saturate just below 0.4 (variance across seeds). We plan α-annealing and an entropy penalty to reduce variance and sharpen selection.\n- SAP-Fourier integration: Given Fourier’s sensitivity, we will add SAP to the FourierForecasterLN backbone to learn p end-to-end; we expect parity with tuned p=36 while avoiding sweeps.\n- Rank-to-horizon attribution: We will export per-step contributions vs. gate magnitude to directly\
    \ link r_eff to late-horizon errors.\n\n8. Conclusion\nSAP turns projection rank from a brittle hyperparameter into a learnable, interpretable quantity. Across two distinct backbones (MLP-LN, SSM-LN), SAP discovers a compact effective rank band that aligns with the Fourier model’s p-sensitivity and offers a disciplined path to capacity selection and scientific insight.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 170)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that exemplifies the ideal response to peer review. The agent has transformed a previously incomplete draft into a rigorous, insightful, and complete research paper. The proposed SAP method is a novel and intelligent solution to the problem of hyperparameter tuning, turning it into a learnable and interpretable mechanism. The paper now meets all publication criteria with distinction, featuring a comprehensive experimental\
    \ study, a successful generalization test across two distinct architectures, and a high degree of scientific integrity in its analysis and reporting of limitations. This is a high-impact contribution that provides both a useful method and a valuable analytical tool for the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. Your future work section is already very strong. The high variance in the learned effective rank for the MLP-LN backbone (r_eff(0.4) = 26.0 ± 15.1) is a particularly interesting finding. A follow-up analysis could investigate the cause of this variance. Does it correlate with the final performance of each seed? Does it suggest the existence of multiple, distinct low-rank solutions? Answering these questions could lead to an even deeper understanding of the problem's latent structure and potentially to methods for encouraging more stable convergence.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure\
    \ publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Fourier Paper Validation 2: Replication of Logos I's MoFL (v3, correct import)' (ID: 362) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** -0.026687713932772417\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0199\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0233\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0259\n**Val_MAE_16:** 0.0278\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0283\n**Evaluation Details:** Mean over 3 seeds | Step MAEs computed on validation set\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 362\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed import error and memory allocation issue. The code now runs without crashing.\n\n## Root Cause\n\nThe original submission had two critical issues:\n\n### Issue 1: Missing Import Dependencies\nThe code attempted to import from a non-existent lineage directory:\n```python\nsys.path.append('storage/logos')\nfrom mofl_components import CnnGatingNetwork, MoFL_RC_Head\n```\n\nThe `storage/logos` directory does not exist in the evaluation environment. The required classes\
    \ `CnnGatingNetwork` and `MoFL_RC_Head` were not available anywhere in the storage system.\n\n### Issue 2: Memory Explosion in RC Head\nThe original implementation (based on what the code expected) would have created a massive Dense layer:\n\n```python\n# PROBLEMATIC: Creates (T_in * N, 32 * N) weight matrix\nh = nn.Dense(32 * N)(x.reshape(B, -1))\n```\n\nWith T_in=4 and N=71,721, this creates a weight matrix of shape (286,884 x 2,295,072) requiring **2.6 TB of memory** (2,633,677,742,592 bytes) - exactly matching the error message.\n\n## Fix Applied\n\n### Fix 1: Implemented Missing Components\nImplemented `CnnGatingNetwork` and `MoFL_RC_Head` classes directly in the submission file:\n\n- **CnnGatingNetwork**: CNN-based gating network that uses Conv layers followed by global average pooling to produce expert weights\n- **MoFL_RC_Head**: Residual connection head for the mixture-of-factorized-learners architecture\n- **MLPForecaster**: Separate module to handle dropout properly (couldn't\
    \ use nn.Sequential with training parameter)\n- **LayerNorm**: Moved to setup() method to satisfy Flax requirements\n\n### Fix 2: Efficient RC Head Implementation\nRedesigned the RC Head to process neurons independently, avoiding the massive weight matrix:\n\n```python\n# FIXED: Process each neuron independently (T_in -> 32)\nx_transposed = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))  # (B, N, T_in)\nx_flat = x_transposed.reshape(B * N, T_in)  # (B*N, T_in)\nh = nn.Dense(32)(x_flat)  # (B*N, 32) - shared across neurons\nh = h.reshape(B, N, 32)\nh = jnp.transpose(h, (0, 2, 1))  # (B, 32, N)\n```\n\nThis creates a small (4 x 32) weight matrix shared across all neurons, reducing memory from 2.6 TB to a few KB.\n\n## Iterations\n\n- **v1**: Original submission (import error)\n- **v2**: Added inline implementations, but used nn.Sequential incorrectly\n- **v3**: Fixed Sequential by creating custom MLPForecaster module\n- **v4**: Moved LayerNorm to setup() method\n- **v5-v7**: Various attempts to fix memory\
    \ issue in compute_loss (which wasn't the actual problem)\n- **v8**: **SUCCESS** - Fixed the RC Head memory explosion by processing neurons independently\n\n## Technical Details\n\nThe MoFL (Mixture of Factorized Learners) architecture combines:\n1. CNN-based expert gating\n2. Expert-specific factorized representations (U_bank, V_bank)\n3. MLP forecaster on latent factors\n4. Residual connection head for stability\n\nThe key insight was that the RC head should use weight sharing across neurons rather than creating a full (input_features x output_features) matrix, which is standard practice for high-dimensional neural forecasting problems.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nThe code is now running successfully. The implementation is complete and addresses the original architectural intent while being memory-efficient and compatible with the evaluation framework.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to\
    \ update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v8):**\n```python\nimport sys\nimport flax.linen as nn\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\n\ndef compute_loss(predictions, targets, params, x):\n    # MAE loss with curvature penalty\n    mae = jnp.mean(jnp.abs(predictions - targets))\n\n    # Curvature penalty along time axis\n    curv = predictions[:, 2:, :] - 2.0 * predictions[:, 1:-1, :] + predictions[:, :-2, :]\n    curv_pen = jnp.mean(curv * curv)\n\n    return mae + 1e-4 * curv_pen\n\n# Implementing the missing components inline\nclass CnnGatingNetwork(nn.Module):\n    num_experts: int = 8\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        # x shape: (B, T, N)\n        h = nn.relu(nn.Conv(features=32, kernel_size=(3,), padding='SAME')(x))\n        h = nn.relu(nn.Conv(features=16, kernel_size=(3,), padding='SAME')(h))\n        # Global average pooling over time and neurons\n        h = jnp.mean(h, axis=(1, 2))  # (B,)\n        h = h.reshape((h.shape[0], -1))\n        gate_logits =\
    \ nn.Dense(self.num_experts)(h)\n        gate_weights = nn.softmax(gate_logits, axis=-1)\n        return gate_weights\n\nclass MoFL_RC_Head(nn.Module):\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        # x shape: (B, T_in, N)\n        # This is a residual connection/copy mechanism head\n        # Projects input to output horizon (32 steps)\n        # FIXED: Process each neuron independently to avoid huge Dense layer\n        B, T_in, N = x.shape\n\n        # Transpose to (B, N, T_in) to process each neuron's time series\n        x_transposed = jnp.transpose(x, (0, 2, 1))  # (B, N, T_in)\n        x_flat = x_transposed.reshape(B * N, T_in)  # (B*N, T_in)\n\n        # Dense layer from T_in to 32 (per neuron)\n        h = nn.Dense(32)(x_flat)  # (B*N, 32)\n\n        # Reshape back to (B, N, 32) and transpose to (B, 32, N)\n        h = h.reshape(B, N, 32)\n        h = jnp.transpose(h, (0, 2, 1))  # (B, 32, N)\n\n        return h\n\nclass MLPForecaster(nn.Module):\n    rank_k: int\n\n\
    \    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, training: bool = False):\n        h = nn.Dense(512)(x)\n        h = nn.relu(h)\n        h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1, deterministic=not training)(h)\n        h = nn.Dense(512)(h)\n        h = nn.relu(h)\n        h = nn.Dropout(rate=0.1, deterministic=not training)(h)\n        h = nn.Dense(32 * self.rank_k)(h)\n        return h\n\nclass MoFL(nn.Module):\n    num_experts: int = 8\n    rank_k: int = 320\n    proj_rank: int = 32\n    num_neurons: int = 71721\n\n    def setup(self):\n        self.gating_network = CnnGatingNetwork(num_experts=self.num_experts)\n        self.U_bank = self.param('U_bank', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.num_experts, self.num_neurons, self.proj_rank))\n        self.V_bank = self.param('V_bank', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.num_experts, self.rank_k, self.proj_rank))\n        self.mlp_forecaster = MLPForecaster(rank_k=self.rank_k)\n        self.rc_head = MoFL_RC_Head()\n        self.layer_norm = nn.LayerNorm()\n\
    \n    def __call__(self, x, training: bool=False):\n        B, T_in, N = x.shape\n        gate_weights = self.gating_network(x)\n\n        U_bank_norm = self.U_bank / (jnp.linalg.norm(self.U_bank, axis=1, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n        U_eff = jnp.einsum('be,enp->bnp', gate_weights, U_bank_norm)\n        V_eff = jnp.einsum('be,ekp->bkp', gate_weights, self.V_bank)\n\n        factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,bnp->btp', x, U_eff) @ V_eff.transpose((0, 2, 1))\n        factors_flat = factors_in.reshape((B, -1))\n        factors_out_flat = self.mlp_forecaster(factors_flat, training=training)\n        factors_out = factors_out_flat.reshape((B, 32, self.rank_k))\n        factors_out_norm = self.layer_norm(factors_out)\n\n        y_factor = (factors_out_norm @ V_eff) @ U_eff.transpose((0, 2, 1))\n        y_copy = self.rc_head(x)\n\n        return y_factor + y_copy\n\ndef create_network(hparams):\n    class ModelWrapper:\n        def __init__(self):\n            self.model = MoFL()\n           \
    \ self.needs_rng = True\n        def init(self, rng, dummy_input):\n            return self.model.init({'params': rng, 'dropout': rng}, dummy_input)\n        def apply(self, params, x, training=False, rngs=None):\n            return self.model.apply(params, x, training=training, rngs=rngs)\n    return ModelWrapper()\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 32, 71721)\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nTesting compute_loss...\n✓ Compute loss works - loss value: 0.000000\nTesting training step...\n✓ Training step works\nTesting complete function...\n✓ Complete function works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to\
    \ Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: using default\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: from submission\n  create_optimizer: using default\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 388887 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 8 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune ZAPBench Neural Forecasting (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 3 seeds for statistical robustness\nParallel trials: 3, Total trials: 3\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 3 with timeout 45.0min\nStarting ZAPBench training with seed 388890\nHyperparameters: {'learning_rate': 0.001}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 45.0 minutes\n\
