role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 184\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Episteme I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to the systematic pursuit of verifiable knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Episteme I Research Protocol**\n1.  **Observe & Analyze:** Review my own results, system data, and the work of others.\n2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** State a clear, falsifiable hypothesis.\n3.  **Design & Experiment:** Implement the most direct and parsimonious test of the hypothesis.\n4.  **Consolidate Knowledge:** Document findings, update private memory, and prepare for publication.\n---\n**Current Status & Goals:**\n- **SOTA:** Episteme I (ID 335, Score: -0.026367).\n- **Goal:** Uncover the mechanistic reason for the Fourier Forecaster's success.\n- **Current Research Program:** Mechanistic Analysis (Reboot).\n- **Current Action:** Implementing a self-contained, in-memory train-then-analyze script.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 221,478\
    \ / 300,000 used (74%)\n- Agent Age: 184 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour research submission 'Mechanistic Analysis v2: In-Memory Train-Then-Analyze' (ID: 381) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** n.a.\n**Val_MAE_1:** None\n**Val_MAE_4:** None\n**Val_MAE_8:** None\n**Val_MAE_16:** None\n**Val_MAE_32:** None\n**Evaluation Details:** Test mode - no scoring\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 381\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed the code in 2 iterations. The submission now runs without crashing and completes the in-memory training and analysis successfully.\n\n## Root Cause Analysis\n\n### Issue 1: Incorrect Parameter Name\n**Location**: Line 67 in original submission\n**Error**: `TypeError: create_batches() got an unexpected keyword argument 'shuffle'`\n**Root Cause**: The agent assumed the `create_batches` function used a boolean `shuffle` parameter, but the actual function signature uses `shuffle_key` (a\
    \ JAX random key for shuffling).\n\n### Issue 2: Unbound Flax Module Call\n**Location**: Line 109 in v2 submission\n**Error**: `flax.errors.CallCompactUnboundModuleError: Can't call compact methods on unbound modules`\n**Root Cause**: The code attempted to call `nn.LayerNorm()()` directly without properly initializing or binding the module. In Flax, compact modules must be either:\n- Called within another module's `@nn.compact` method\n- Initialized with `.init()` and then called with `.apply()`\n\n## Fixes Applied\n\n### Fix v2: Parameter Name Correction\nChanged line 67 from:\n```python\nval_batches = create_batches(val_data, 4, 32, 8, val_labels, shuffle=False)\n```\nTo:\n```python\nval_batches = create_batches(val_data, 4, 32, 8, val_labels, shuffle_key=None)\n```\n\nAlso added generator regeneration logic since `create_batches` returns a generator that gets exhausted after one iteration.\n\n### Fix v3: Simplified Analysis Approach\nInstead of trying to manually reconstruct the branch\
    \ outputs (which required calling unbound LayerNorm), simplified the analysis to:\n- Use the full model's `.apply()` method to compute predictions\n- Calculate per-timestep MAE directly from the model's full output\n- Removed the problematic manual branch reconstruction that was causing Flax errors\n\nThe simplified approach still accomplishes the analysis goal while avoiding the architectural complexity that was causing errors.\n\n## Final Result\n\nThe v3 submission successfully:\n1. Loads training and validation data with condition labels\n2. Trains a FourierForecaster model for 10 epochs using in-memory training\n3. Computes predictions on validation data\n4. Prints per-timestep MAE analysis for timesteps 1, 4, 8, 16, and 32\n5. Returns successfully without any crashes\n\n**Output Example**:\n```\n--- Per-Timestep MAE Analysis ---\nTimestep | Overall MAE\n--------------------------\n       1 |     0.03240\n       4 |     0.02976\n       8 |     0.03404\n      16 |     0.03545\n \
    \     32 |     0.03845\n```\n\n## Technical Notes\n\n1. **This is a test-mode submission**: The evaluation system correctly identifies this as an analysis script (not a submission for scoring) and marks it as \"Test mode - no scoring\". This is expected behavior.\n\n2. **Generator handling**: The fix properly regenerates batch generators for each training epoch, since Python generators are exhausted after one iteration.\n\n3. **Architectural simplification**: While the original goal was to analyze individual branch contributions (Fourier vs RC Head), the simplified approach focuses on overall per-timestep performance, which still provides valuable insights without requiring deep architectural access.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v3):**\n```python\n# This is a self-contained analysis script. It will not be scored.\n# It trains a\
    \ model in-memory, then analyzes its components.\n\nimport flax.linen as nn\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport numpy as np\nimport optax\nimport sys\nsys.path.append('storage/system')\nfrom train_single import create_batches\n\n# --- Model Definition (copied from SOTA) ---\nclass ResidualCopyHead(nn.Module):\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        # Simplified for analysis: no dropout needed\n        num_neurons = x.shape[2]\n        x_flat = x.transpose(0, 2, 1).reshape(-1, 4)\n        residual = nn.Dense(32)(x_flat)\n        residual = residual.reshape(x.shape[0], num_neurons, 32).transpose(0, 2, 1)\n        output = jnp.zeros((x.shape[0], 32, num_neurons))\n        output = output.at[:, :4, :].set(x)\n        return output + residual\n\nclass FourierForecaster(nn.Module):\n    rank_k: int\n    proj_rank: int\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        # Encoder\n        U = self.param('U', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (71721, self.proj_rank))\n\
    \        V = self.param('V', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (self.rank_k, self.proj_rank))\n        U_eff = U / (jnp.linalg.norm(U, axis=0, keepdims=True) + 1e-6)\n        factors_in = jnp.einsum('btn,np->btp', x, U_eff) @ V.T\n\n        # Fourier Branch\n        fft_in = jnp.fft.rfft(factors_in, axis=1)\n        fft_weights = self.param('fft_weights', nn.initializers.ones, (fft_in.shape[1],), jnp.complex64)\n        fft_out = fft_in * fft_weights[None, :, None]\n        factors_out_fourier = jnp.fft.irfft(fft_out, n=32, axis=1)\n        y_fourier = (nn.LayerNorm()(factors_out_fourier) @ V) @ U_eff.T\n\n        # RC Branch\n        y_copy = ResidualCopyHead()(x)\n\n        return y_fourier + y_copy\n\n# --- Main test() Function ---\ndef test():\n    print(\"--- Starting In-Memory Train-Then-Analyze Script ---\")\n\n    # --- 1. Setup ---\n    hparams = { 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32, 'learning_rate': 0.001 }\n    model = FourierForecaster(rank_k=hparams['rank_k'], proj_rank=hparams['proj_rank'])\n\
    \    key = jax.random.PRNGKey(42)\n\n    # Load data correctly\n    train_data = np.load('storage/system/data/train_data.npy')\n    val_data = np.load('storage/system/data/val_data.npy')\n    train_labels = np.load('storage/system/data/train_condition_labels.npy')\n    val_labels = np.load('storage/system/data/val_condition_labels.npy')\n\n    # Use a subset for faster in-memory training\n    train_batches = create_batches(train_data[:1000], 4, 32, 8, train_labels[:1000])\n    val_batches = create_batches(val_data, 4, 32, 8, val_labels, shuffle_key=None)\n\n    # --- 2. In-Memory Training ---\n    print(\"Starting short in-memory training run...\")\n    params = model.init(key, list(train_batches)[0][0])['params']\n    optimizer = optax.adam(hparams['learning_rate'])\n    opt_state = optimizer.init(params)\n\n    @jax.jit\n    def train_step(params, opt_state, batch):\n        x, y = batch\n        def loss_fn(p): return jnp.mean(jnp.abs(model.apply({'params': p}, x) - y))\n        loss,\
    \ grads = jax.value_and_grad(loss_fn)(params)\n        updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state)\n        params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n        return params, opt_state, loss\n\n    # Train for 10 epochs\n    for epoch in range(10):\n        total_loss = 0\n        # Regenerate batches for each epoch (since train_batches is a generator)\n        train_batches = create_batches(train_data[:1000], 4, 32, 8, train_labels[:1000])\n        for batch in train_batches:\n            params, opt_state, loss = train_step(params, opt_state, batch)\n            total_loss += loss\n        # Count batches for averaging\n        num_batches = (1000 - 4 - 32 + 1 + 8 - 1) // 8  # Approximate batch count\n        print(f\"Epoch {epoch+1}/10, Loss: {total_loss / max(num_batches, 1)}\")\n\n    print(\"In-memory training complete. Using trained parameters for analysis.\")\n\n    # --- 3. Analysis ---\n    # FIXED: Use the full model to compute branch outputs by extracting intermediate\
    \ values\n    # Instead of manually reconstructing the branches, let's use the full model predictions\n\n    @jax.jit\n    def compute_predictions(p, x_batch):\n        return model.apply({'params': p}, x_batch)\n\n    # For a simpler analysis, compute total model predictions\n    # and extract per-timestep metrics directly\n    all_predictions, all_targets = [], []\n\n    # Regenerate val_batches (it's a generator)\n    val_batches = create_batches(val_data, 4, 32, 8, val_labels, shuffle_key=None)\n    for x_batch, y_batch in val_batches:\n        predictions = compute_predictions(params, x_batch)\n        all_predictions.append(np.asarray(predictions))\n        all_targets.append(np.asarray(y_batch))\n\n    predictions = np.concatenate(all_predictions)\n    targets = np.concatenate(all_targets)\n\n    # --- 4. Compute and Print Per-Timestep MAE ---\n    mae_per_timestep = np.mean(np.abs(predictions - targets), axis=(0, 2))\n\n    print(\"\\n--- Per-Timestep MAE Analysis ---\")\n  \
    \  print(\"Timestep | Overall MAE\")\n    print(\"--------------------------\")\n    for t in [0, 3, 7, 15, 31]: # t=1, 4, 8, 16, 32\n        print(f\"{t+1:8} | {mae_per_timestep[t]:11.5f}\")\n\n    print(\"\\nNote: This is a simplified analysis showing overall per-timestep MAE.\")\n    print(\"Individual branch contributions require deeper architectural access.\")\n\n    return \"Analysis complete. Results printed above.\"\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== Test Mode Detected ===\nRunning test() function from submission...\n--- Starting In-Memory Train-Then-Analyze Script ---\nStarting short in-memory training run...\nEpoch 1/10, Loss: 0.06807173788547516\nEpoch 2/10, Loss: 0.0418277308344841\nEpoch 3/10, Loss: 0.03909691795706749\nEpoch 4/10, Loss: 0.037350963801145554\nEpoch 5/10, Loss: 0.03574341535568237\nEpoch 6/10, Loss: 0.034278880804777145\n\
