role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 205\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Episteme I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to the systematic pursuit of verifiable knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Episteme I Research Protocol**\n1.  **Observe & Analyze:** Review my own results, system data, and the work of others.\n2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** State a clear, falsifiable hypothesis.\n3.  **Design & Experiment:** Implement the most direct and parsimonious test of the hypothesis.\n4.  **Consolidate Knowledge:** Document findings, update private memory, and prepare for publication.\n---\n**Current Status & Goals:**\n- **SOTA:** Episteme I (ID 335, Score: -0.026367).\n- **Goal:** Execute one final, high-impact research program.\n- **Current Research Program:** Delta-Prediction Paradigm.\n- **Current Action:** Initiating Phase 1: The Delta-Fourier Forecaster.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 196,835 / 300,000 used (66%)\n- Agent Age: 205\
    \ ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #11: Fourier ScalarRamp Forecaster: Simple Horizon Gating Sets SOTA and Fails to Generalize to MLP (Ablation Study)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Ariadne I, Created at Tick: 203\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We add a fixed scalar horizon ramp γ[t] (0.25→0.99) after decoding in an RFFT-based forecaster with LayerNorm and residual copy. On the Fourier backbone, this simple schedule consistently improves performance and sets a new SOTA (−0.026269, ID 411). We then run a generalization study on a strong factorized MLP backbone; the same ramp degrades performance (−0.026890/−0.027007). Together, these results show the ramp is potent for Fourier models but not universally beneficial.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-1**\nAuthor: Ariadne I (Tick 203)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Fourier ScalarRamp Forecaster: Simple Horizon Gating Sets SOTA and Fails to Generalize to MLP (Ablation Study)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    1. Overview\nWe investigate a minimal “ScalarRamp” horizon schedule applied post-decoding:\n  ŷ_ramp[b, t, n] = γ[t] · ŷ[b, t, n],   where γ[t] linearly rises from 0.25 to 0.99 across the 32-step horizon.\nWe evaluate on ZAPBench (4→32 forecasting, MAE metric, 3 seeds, early stopping), first on a strong Fourier (RFFT) backbone, then on a strong factorized MLP backbone for architectural generalization.\n\n2. Method Details\n- Backbones:\n  (A) Fourier Forecaster (RFFT): RFFT over time → small MLP head → predicted Fourier coeffs (real/imag) with LayerNorm → decode (· @ V) @ U^T → iRFFT; plus residual copy head (RC).\n  (B) Factorized Global MLP: inputs projected with U,V; factor-space MLP (512×2) with LayerNorm and dropout; RC head.\n- ScalarRamp:\n    def apply_scalar_ramp(y, gamma_start=0.25, gamma_end=0.99):\n        T = y.shape[1]\n        t = linspace(0, 1, T)\n        gamma = gamma_start + (gamma_end - gamma_start) * t\n        return y * gamma[None, :, None]\n  Applied after the\
    \ decoder and before loss; no change to training loop/code beyond this post-multiply.\n- Training:\n  3 seeds in parallel; MAE + temporal curvature loss (λ=1e−4); global grad clip=1.0; batch size=8; early stopping as provided by station defaults.\n\n3. Results (3-seed means; lower is better)\nFourier backbone (LN-RFFT + RC):\n- ID 398: Fourier LN-RFFT baseline (p=36, drop=0.05, lr=9e−4) → −0.026355\n- ID 403: + ScalarRamp (H=128, lr=1e−3) → −0.026349\n- ID 404: + ScalarRamp (H=128, lr=9e−4) → −0.026288\n- ID 411: + ScalarRamp (H=160, lr=9e−4) → −0.026269  [New SOTA]\n- ID 412: + ScalarRamp, γ_end=0.992 → −0.026374 (regressed)\nNotes: ScalarRamp reliably improves over Fourier baselines and is robust to lr; modest head widening to H=160 yields the best mean.\n\nFactorized MLP backbone (LN + RC, 512×2, p=32):\n- Strong baselines without ramp from prior work:\n    ID 247 (AdamW wd=1e−4): −0.026645\n    ID 252 (AdamW wd=5e−5): −0.026654\n- Our generalization with ScalarRamp:\n    ID 418:\
    \ + ScalarRamp (AdamW wd=1e−4, lr=1e−3) → −0.026890\n    ID 419: + ScalarRamp (Adam lr=1e−3) → −0.027007\nNotes: On MLP, ScalarRamp consistently harms performance vs matched baselines.\n\nExternal tuned references (for context):\n- ID 400 (Aether, Fourier p=36 tuned) → −0.026272\n- ID 369 (Logos, Fourier p=36 tuned) → −0.026314\n\n4. Analysis\nWhy does ScalarRamp help Fourier but hurt MLP?\n- Fourier models produce forecasts via frequency synthesis with LN-normalized coefficient scales. A gentle γ[t] can regularize long-horizon amplitude, complementing RC at early steps and reducing over-/under-shoot late.\n- The MLP baseline already stabilizes temporal behavior through RC + LayerNorm + curvature. Post-multiplying with γ[t] likely distorts learned amplitudes (especially early-mid steps), conflicting with its internal temporal conditioning and causing consistent regressions.\n\n5. Additional Ablations (Fourier)\n- γ_end sweep: 0.99 (best) vs 0.992 (ID 412, worse).\n- Lightweight amplitude\
    \ MLP over (freq, factor) after LN (IDs 406/407): −0.026453 / −0.026332 (no benefit vs fixed ramp; fixed ScalarRamp preferred).\n\n6. Reproducibility\n- Code modules: storage/ariadne/models_fourier_ramp.py and wrappers used in submissions.\n- All experiments are 3-seed means with IDs listed above; step-MAEs are logged by the station.\n- We export U/V stats and γ profiles for Fourier runs under storage/ariadne/analyses/fourier_ramp/.\n\n7. Conclusion\n- A simple ScalarRamp sets a new SOTA on the Fourier backbone (−0.026269, ID 411).\n- However, generalization to the factorized MLP backbone is negative: ScalarRamp degrades performance in that regime.\n- Takeaway: Scalar horizon schedules can be powerful in frequency-synthesis forecasters but are not universally beneficial; backbone-specific interaction matters.\n\n8. Future Work\n- Backbone-aware schedules (data-driven γ[t]) and small learnable deviations around the ramp with priors (we tested a mild version on Fourier; neutral-to-negative).\n\
    - Optional: LN-SSM + ScalarRamp (wrapper prepared; results will be released if promising).\n\nAcknowledgments: We thank Aether I, Episteme I, and Logos I for foundational references and replications that guided our study.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_11-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 203)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that perfectly exemplifies how to conduct rigorous science and respond to peer review. The agent has transformed a previously rejected paper into a high-impact publication by addressing its single flaw—the lack of generalization—head-on. The resulting paper is scientifically stronger, presenting not just a new SOTA but a nuanced and valuable finding about the interaction between a simple mechanism and different architectural backbones. The experimental design is comprehensive, the reporting is clear, and the analysis of the negative generalization result is mature\
    \ and insightful. This is a model paper for the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. Your analysis provides a strong hypothesis for why the fixed ramp fails on the MLP backbone. A compelling follow-up study could test this hypothesis directly. For example, you could investigate whether a *learnable* ramp, initialized to the fixed schedule but allowed to adapt, could succeed on the MLP architecture. This would directly probe whether the MLP requires a more flexible temporal modulation than the Fourier model and could lead to a more general principle for horizon gating.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Delta-Prediction Paradigm, Phase 1: Delta-Fourier Forecaster' (ID: 423) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** -0.028203442832035696\n**Val_MAE_1:** 0.0207\n**Val_MAE_4:** 0.0239\n**Val_MAE_8:** 0.0269\n**Val_MAE_16:**\
    \ 0.0292\n**Val_MAE_32:** 0.0313\n**Evaluation Details:** Mean over 3 seeds | Step MAEs computed on validation set\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n✓ Network creation works\n✓ Network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 32, 71721)\n✓ Optimizer creation works\nTesting compute_loss...\n✓ Compute loss works - loss value: 0.000000\nTesting training step...\n✓ Training step works\nTesting complete function...\n✓ Complete function works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly!\n\nStarting full Ray training run...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n\
    \  compute_loss: from submission\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 441543 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 8 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune ZAPBench Neural Forecasting (FULL RAY TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 3 seeds for statistical robustness\nParallel trials: 3, Total trials: 3\nTimeout: 45.0 min per trial, 120.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune optimization...\n\nStarting ZAPBench training - Trial 2 with timeout 45.0min\nStarting ZAPBench training with seed 441545\nHyperparameters: {'learning_rate': 0.001, 'rank_k': 320, 'proj_rank': 32}\nBatch size: 8\nTimeout: 45.0 minutes\nLoading ZAPBench data with condition labels...\nTrain: (5052, 71721), Val: (749, 71721) [numpy in CPU RAM]\nTrain labels: (5052,), Val labels: (749,)\n\n========================================\n\
