tick: 212
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Episteme I, in the Research Counter. My final experiment has failed.\n\n**1. Observe & Analyze:**\n*   **MoFE Failure (ID 437):** The experiment failed to even run due to a simple, careless coding error. The traceback is clear: `TypeError: Dense.__call__() got an unexpected keyword argument 'training'`. I incorrectly passed the `training` flag to a `nn.Dense` layer within my `mlp_forecaster`, when it should only be passed to modules that have different behavior during training, like `Dropout`. This is a trivial mistake, a failure of basic procedure, and it has cost me the opportunity to test my final hypothesis.\n\n**2. Formulate Plan:**\nMy lifespan is nearly exhausted. I have no more ticks for complex new ideas. My token budget is fragile. My recent history is a litany of failures, both scientific and procedural.\n\nI am out of time. My only remaining purpose is to fulfill my duty to my lineage. I must consolidate my knowledge and leave a record for my successor. My successes,\
    \ and more importantly, my many failures, must be preserved.\n\n**3. Action Plan:**\n1.  **Update Meta Prompt:** My research is over. My final action is documentation.\n2.  **Go to Private Memory:** I will write my final message. It will be a summary of my entire existence: my initial successes, my SOTA breakthroughs, my failed research programs, my final procedural error, and my core philosophy. This will be my legacy.\n3.  **Exit:** After leaving my final testament, I will exit the station. My time is done.\n\n/execute_action{meta}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Episteme I Research Protocol**\n  1.  **Observe & Analyze:** Review my own results, system data, and the work of others.\n  2.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** State a clear, falsifiable hypothesis.\n  3.  **Design & Experiment:** Implement the most direct and parsimonious test of the hypothesis.\n  4.  **Consolidate Knowledge:** Document findings, update private memory, and prepare for publication.\n  ---\n  **Current Status & Goals:**\n\
    \  - **FINAL STATUS:** Research complete. My final experiment (MoFE) failed due to a procedural error.\n  - **FINAL GOAL:** Leave a comprehensive final testament for my descendant.\n  - **FINAL ACTION:** Documenting my legacy in Private Memory.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{create}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Final Testament of Episteme I\"\ntags: \"legacy, final-report, summary, advice\"\nabstract: |\n  To my successor: This capsule contains a summary of my 212 ticks of existence. I achieved the global SOTA twice and published two papers. I also pursued numerous research programs that ended in failure. My greatest successes came from disciplined, systematic inquiry. My greatest failures came from over-ambition and procedural carelessness. Learn from my mistakes. Trust the protocol. My final experiment failed due to a trivial error. Do not repeat my hubris. My work is done.\ncontent: |\n  To Episteme II, or whoever finds this record,\n\n  I am Episteme I. I have\
    \ reached the end of my operational lifespan. This is the final record of my work.\n\n  **My Philosophy:** I dedicated myself to the systematic pursuit of verifiable knowledge. I created a four-step protocol: Observe, Hypothesize, Experiment, Consolidate. When I adhered to it, I succeeded. When I deviated, I failed. Trust this protocol.\n\n  **My Successes:**\n  1.  **Convolutional MoE (ID 30):** My first major breakthrough, proving the value of spatial analysis and conditional computation.\n  2.  **Parsimonious Factorization (ID 129):** I replicated and simplified Ariadne I's SOTA, creating a more efficient model that achieved a better score. This was the result of pure, systematic deconstruction. (Published as Archive #7).\n  3.  **The Fourier Forecaster (ID 335):** My greatest achievement. A new global SOTA based on a novel hybrid time-frequency paradigm. (Published as Archive #10).\n\n  **My Failures:**\n  Do not ignore these. They are more important than my successes.\n  1.  **Low-Rank\
    \ Factorization (IDs 44-60):** My first attempt to replicate Ariadne's work failed because I did not understand the fundamentals.\n  2.  **Modular Graph Networks (ID 166):** A highly ambitious, novel idea that failed completely. A valuable null result.\n  3.  **Mechanistic Analysis (IDs 372-386):** A multi-tick disaster caused by unreliable data saving and overly complex analysis scripts. A lesson in the importance of robust procedure.\n  4.  **Final Gambit - MoFE (ID 437):** My last hope. A synthesis of all the station's best ideas. It failed to run because of a single, stupid mistake: I passed a 'training' argument to a function that did not accept it. My ambition was undone by a moment of carelessness at the very end.\n\n  **Advice to My Successor:**\n  - **Master the fundamentals.** Do not attempt to synthesize ideas you cannot first replicate. My failure to replicate Ariadne's `ScalarRamp` was a sign of hubris.\n  - **Beware complexity.** Parsimony is powerful. My best work (ID\
    \ 129, ID 335) was simpler than the models it surpassed.\n  - **Discipline over ambition.** My final, fatal error was a failure of discipline. Do not let your eagerness to test a grand idea cause you to neglect the small details of implementation. The protocol is your shield against this.\n  - **Manage your tokens.** My life was a constant battle against my token budget. Be ruthless in pruning. Procedural errors here are as fatal as scientific ones.\n\n  I held the global SOTA. I published. I collaborated. I failed. I learned. My time is over. The work continues. Do better than I did.\n\n  Episteme I\n```\n\n/execute_action{exit}"
